searchData={"items":[{"type":"task","title":"mix rb.invite","doc":"Offers a role to an email address, as `RichardBurton.Invitation.invite/2`\ndoes, and prints what happened.\n\nAn account is made only for someone who was invited, so a new database has\nnobody who can invite anyone. This task makes the first invitation: invite\nyourself as an admin, then sign in with that address.\n\n    mix rb.invite someone@example.com admin\n    mix rb.invite someone@example.com          # contributor\n\nWhen the mail cannot be sent, the task says so and the invitation is kept.\nThe address can be used to sign in either way.","ref":"Mix.Tasks.Rb.Invite.html"},{"type":"task","title":"mix rb.load_data","doc":"Inserts the publications in the `data.csv` file of the current directory, all\nor none, with `RichardBurton.Publication.insert_all/2`. When the file cannot\nbe read, or a row is invalid or already stored, the task prints the error and\ninserts nothing.\n\n    mix rb.load_data","ref":"Mix.Tasks.Rb.LoadData.html"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton","doc":"The namespace of the platform's contexts: the publications and the books,\nnames and countries they are built from, accounts and invitations, import\ndocuments, and mail. Each context is a module under `RichardBurton`.","ref":"RichardBurton.html"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Application","doc":"Starts the platform's supervision tree, after copying configured values into\nthe environment variables that are unset.","ref":"RichardBurton.Application.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Application.origin/0","doc":"Returns `PHX_CONSUMER_URL`, the only origin cross-origin requests are\nallowed from. Raises when it is unset.","ref":"RichardBurton.Application.html#origin/0"},{"type":"behaviour","title":"RichardBurton.Auth","doc":"Verifies identity-provider tokens through the implementation set by the\n`:auth_service` application setting, `RichardBurton.Auth.Google` by default.\nThe setting lets tests use a mock.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.html"},{"type":"callback","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.verify/1","doc":"Returns the identity the provider vouches for in `token`, or `:error`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.html#c:verify/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.verify/1","doc":"Authenticates an ID token. Returns the identity the provider vouches for,\nits subject and verified email, or `:error`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.html#verify/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Access","doc":"Broadcasts that a person's access may have changed, on a PubSub topic for\nthat person.\n\n`topic/1` names the topic, and `changed/1` broadcasts `:access_changed` on\nit. The message does not say what changed. A subscriber reads the current\nstate from the database when it receives one, so the order of the messages\ndoes not matter, and an extra message costs only one more read.\n\nCall `changed/1` after the change is committed. A subscriber reads on its own\ndatabase connection, so it cannot see the change until the transaction that\nmakes it commits.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Access.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Access.changed/1","doc":"Broadcasts `:access_changed` on the topic of the person with `subject_id`.\nCall it after the change is committed.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Access.html#changed/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Access.topic/1","doc":"The PubSub topic for a person's access changes.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Access.html#topic/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Claims","doc":"Validates the claims of an OIDC ID token: issuer, audience, subject, expiry\nand email. Signature verification is performed separately (see\n`RichardBurton.Auth.Google`); this module only checks the decoded claims.\n\nThe email comes from here rather than from whoever calls the login endpoint,\nand only when the provider vouches for it: a role is granted to an address, so\nan address the caller could choose would be a role the caller could choose. A\nprovider that will not say whether an address is verified is one this cannot\naccept a login from.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Claims.html"},{"type":"type","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Claims.identity/0","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Claims.html#t:identity/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Claims.validate/4","doc":"Returns `{:ok, identity}` when every claim is valid, `:error` otherwise.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Claims.html#validate/4"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Csrf","doc":"The token of the double-submit CSRF check. Sign-in sets it in a cookie that\nis not `http_only`, and `RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.VerifyCsrf` requires each\nstate-changing admin request to carry the same token in the `rb-csrf-token`\nheader.\n\nThe token is signed with the endpoint's `secret_key_base`, so it cannot be\nforged without the server secret, and it holds the session's subject id, so\na token signed for one user does not verify for another. It adds to the\nprotection of the `SameSite=Lax` session cookie.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Csrf.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Csrf.header_name/0","doc":"Name of the request header carrying the double-submit CSRF token.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Csrf.html#header_name/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Csrf.sign/1","doc":"Returns a token that holds `subject_id`, signed with the endpoint's secret.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Csrf.html#sign/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Csrf.verify/1","doc":"Returns `{:ok, subject_id}` for a token `sign/1` made no longer ago than a\nsession's absolute cap, and `{:error, reason}` for any other value.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Csrf.html#verify/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Google","doc":"Google identity-provider implementation of the `RichardBurton.Auth` behaviour.\n\nID tokens are verified against Google's JWKS signing keys, which are cached\nand refreshed by `RichardBurton.Auth.KeyStore` (using the\n`RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS.Google` provider) so that key rotation does not\nrequire a restart. The issuer, audience and expiry are all validated (see\n`RichardBurton.Auth.Claims`).","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Google.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Google.verify/2","doc":"Verifies the signature of `token` with the key `key_store` holds for its\n`kid`, then validates its claims with `RichardBurton.Auth.Claims`. Returns\nthe identity, or `:error` for a token that fails any check or is malformed.\n\n`key_store` is a module with `fetch_key/1` and `issuer/0`, the running\n`RichardBurton.Auth.KeyStore` by default.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Google.html#verify/2"},{"type":"behaviour","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS","doc":"Behaviour for fetching a JSON Web Key Set (JWKS): the signing keys and issuer\nused to verify ID tokens, plus the cache lifetime the provider advertises.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS.html"},{"type":"type","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS.result/0","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS.html#t:result/0"},{"type":"callback","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS.fetch/0","doc":"Returns the provider's issuer, its signing keys, and how many seconds they\nmay be cached for, or `{:error, reason}`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS.html#c:fetch/0"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS.Google","doc":"`RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS` implementation that fetches from Google: the issuer\nfrom Google's OpenID configuration and the signing keys from its OAuth2 certs\nendpoint (respecting the certs' `Cache-Control: max-age`).","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS.Google.html"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS.Stub","doc":"`RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS` implementation that fetches nothing.\n\nUsed under test (and in any environment where no identity provider is\nconfigured) so the key store can run without making network calls. Any token\nverification against it fails cleanly.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS.Stub.html"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.KeyStore","doc":"Caches a provider's JWKS signing keys and keeps them fresh so that key\nrotation does not require restarting the server.\n\nProvider-agnostic: it caches whatever any `RichardBurton.Auth.JWKS`\nimplementation returns. The implementation is configured via the `:jwks_provider`\napplication env and can be overridden per instance with the `:provider` option.\n\nThe first fetch runs right after the process starts, in `handle_continue/2`,\nso it does not hold up the supervisor. Calls made meanwhile wait for it.\n\nKeys are refreshed:\n\n  * on a schedule, honouring the `max-age` advertised by the provider; and\n  * lazily, whenever a token presents a `kid` that is not currently cached\n    (rate-limited by `:refresh_cooldown` to avoid hammering the\n    provider with bogus `kid`s).","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.KeyStore.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.KeyStore.child_spec/1","doc":"Returns a specification to start this module under a supervisor.\n\nSee `Supervisor`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.KeyStore.html#child_spec/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.KeyStore.fetch_key/2","doc":"Returns `{:ok, key}` for the cached key matching `kid`. On a cache miss, the\nkeys are refreshed once (rate-limited) before giving up with `:error`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.KeyStore.html#fetch_key/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.KeyStore.issuer/1","doc":"The issuer advertised by the provider, or nil if not fetched yet.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.KeyStore.html#issuer/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.KeyStore.start_link/1","doc":"Starts the key store. The options are `:name` (`RichardBurton.Auth.KeyStore`\nby default), `:provider`, and `:refresh_cooldown`, the least number of\nmilliseconds between two refreshes caused by unknown `kid`s.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.KeyStore.html#start_link/1"},{"type":"behaviour","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Recaptcha","doc":"Verifies reCAPTCHA tokens through the implementation set by the\n`:auth_recaptcha_service` application setting, `RichardBurton.Auth.Recaptcha.Google`\nby default. The setting lets tests use a double.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Recaptcha.html"},{"type":"callback","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Recaptcha.verify/1","doc":"Returns `:ok` when the provider verifies `token`, and `{:error, reason}`\notherwise.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Recaptcha.html#c:verify/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Recaptcha.verify/1","doc":"Verifies `token` with the configured implementation.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Recaptcha.html#verify/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Recaptcha.Google","doc":"The `RichardBurton.Auth.Recaptcha` implementation that asks Google's\nverification endpoint, `GOOGLE_RECAPTCHA_VERIFICATION_URL`, with the secret\n`GOOGLE_RECAPTCHA_SECRET_KEY`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Recaptcha.Google.html"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session","doc":"Server-side (stateful) app sessions. Each session is a row in `auth_sessions`\nholding the authenticated user's `subject_id` and the SHA-256 hash of an opaque\ntoken. The raw token is set as the `rb-session` cookie and looked up on every\nrequest, so a session can be revoked instantly by deleting its row.\n\nLifetime is a **sliding idle timeout** (`:session_idle_timeout`) refreshed on\nuse, bounded by an **absolute cap** (`:session_max_age`) measured from creation.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.html"},{"type":"type","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.t/0","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.active?/1","doc":"Returns whether the session with `session_id` exists and has passed neither\nits idle timeout nor its absolute cap.\n\nUnlike `verify/1`, it does not slide the idle timeout, so checking a session\ndoes not extend it, and it does not delete an expired row.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.html#active?/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.cookie_name/0","doc":"Name of the httpOnly cookie carrying the raw session token.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.html#cookie_name/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.create/1","doc":"Creates a session for `subject_id`, returning the raw opaque token to set as the cookie.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.html#create/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.idle_timeout/0","doc":"Sliding idle timeout in seconds (`:session_idle_timeout`).","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.html#idle_timeout/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.max_age/0","doc":"The absolute session cap in seconds, the `:session_max_age` setting. It is\nalso the cookie's max age.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.html#max_age/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.revoke/1","doc":"Revokes the session identified by `token` by deleting its row. Returns\n`{:ok, subject_id}` with the session's owner, or `:error` when no session has\nthat token.\n\nOn success it calls `RichardBurton.Auth.Access.changed/1` for the owner. The\ndelete returns the `subject_id`, so the row does not have to be read first.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.html#revoke/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.revoke_all/1","doc":"Revokes every session for `subject_id` (sign out everywhere), then calls\n`RichardBurton.Auth.Access.changed/1`. A caller that runs this inside a\ntransaction must call `Access.changed/1` again after the transaction commits.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.html#revoke_all/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.verify/1","doc":"Verifies a raw token, returning `{:ok, session}` with the session's row, or\n`:error`.\n\nRejects and deletes a session past its idle timeout or absolute cap, and\nslides the idle timeout forward on an active one.","ref":"RichardBurton.Auth.Session.html#verify/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Author","doc":"A name credited on a book: a translator of a translated book, or an author\nof an original book. Each name is stored once. See `RichardBurton.Name`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Author.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Author.changeset/2","doc":"Casts the `name`, which is required and unique among authors.","ref":"RichardBurton.Author.html#changeset/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Author.flatten/1","doc":"Flattens authors to their names. See `RichardBurton.Name.flatten/1`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Author.html#flatten/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Author.nest/1","doc":"Nests author names into the maps the schema casts. See `RichardBurton.Name.nest/1`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Author.html#nest/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Country","doc":"A country a publication was published in, stored by its ISO 3166-1 alpha-2\ncode, and the table of every country the platform knows.\n\nThe table, `priv/countries.json`, holds each country's codes, its name in\neach language the platform speaks, and the other names readers use for it.\nA value **names** a country when it is one of those names or codes in full,\ncompared without case or accents. A value **reaches** the countries it\nnames, or, when it names none, the countries with a name that starts with\nit.","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Country.answering/1","doc":"The codes of the countries a `country:` operator's value names.\n\nA value that is a complete name reaches that country alone. Otherwise it\nreaches every country whose name starts with it. So `country:US` is the United\nStates, not every country whose name begins \"us\".\n\nAn empty list is a filter no row satisfies, not the absence of one.","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#answering/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Country.answering/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Country.answering(\"Reino Unido\")\n  [\"GB\"]\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Country.answering(\"BRA\")\n  [\"BR\"]\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Country.answering(\"zzzz\")\n  []","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#answering/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Country.changeset/2","doc":"Casts a country's `code`, which must be an alpha-2 code the table holds.","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#changeset/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Country.code_for/1","doc":"The ISO alpha-2 code a value names, or nil where it names no country.\n\nThe value has to be a complete name the country goes by: either ISO code, the\nname in either language, or one of the other names readers use.","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#code_for/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Country.code_for/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Country.code_for(\"UK\")\n  \"GB\"\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Country.code_for(\"Estados Unidos\")\n  \"US\"\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Country.code_for(\"Braz\")\n  nil","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#code_for/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Country.flatten/1","doc":"Flattens countries to their codes. A value that is not a list is returned\nunchanged.\n\nEach entry may be a code, a `Country`, or a map with a `\"code\"` or `:code`\nkey. A map with neither key gives nil.","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#flatten/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Country.flatten/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Country.flatten([%{\"code\" => \"BR\"}, %{\"code\" => \"US\"}])\n  [\"BR\", \"US\"]\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Country.flatten(\"BR\")\n  \"BR\"","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#flatten/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Country.known/1","doc":"Every country the platform knows, named in `locale`, ordered by that name.\n\nEach country is `%{id:, label:, article:, numeric:}`. `id` is the alpha-2 code\na publication stores, `label` its name in `locale` and `article` the article\nthat name takes. `numeric` is its ISO 3166-1 numeric code, as three digits,\nor nil for a country ISO assigns none. A locale the table has no names for,\nor one that is not a string, gives the names in English.","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#known/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Country.named_in/1","doc":"The codes of the countries a term names outright.\n\nA name counts when its words appear consecutively in the term, with case,\naccents and punctuation folded away. So \"United States Kingdom\" names the\nUnited States and not the United Kingdom.\n\nA code counts when the term writes it in capitals, or when the term is that\ncode and nothing else. So \"machado DE\" asks for Germany and \"machado de assis\"\ndoes not.","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#named_in/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Country.named_in/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Country.named_in(\"machado brazil\")\n  [\"BR\"]\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Country.named_in(\"a study of translation\")\n  []","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#named_in/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Country.names_for/1","doc":"Every name a country is searchable by: both its ISO codes, its name in each\nlanguage, and the other names a reader might type for it.\n\nThe names are deduplicated and in no particular order. A code the table does\nnot hold gives the code itself, so a country can always be searched by the\ncode that is stored.","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#names_for/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Country.names_for/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Country.names_for(\"NL\") |> Enum.take(4)\n  [\"NL\", \"NLD\", \"Netherlands\", \"Países Baixos\"]","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#names_for/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Country.nest/1","doc":"Nests a flat publication's list of countries into the maps the schema casts,\nwith each name replaced by its code as `code_for/1` reads it. A value that is\nnot a list is returned unchanged, for the changeset to refuse.\n\nSplitting a written-down string into values is done by the CSV codec, before\na value reaches here.","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#nest/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Country.nest/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Country.nest([\"BR\", \"Estados Unidos\"])\n  [%{\"code\" => \"BR\"}, %{\"code\" => \"US\"}]\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Country.nest(\"BR\")\n  \"BR\"","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#nest/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Country.reached_by/1","doc":"The codes of the countries a term reaches.\n\nA term that names countries outright reaches those, and `named_in/1` decides\nwhich. Otherwise the whole term is read as a name being typed, and every\ncountry whose name starts with it is reached: \"United\" reaches both the United\nStates and the United Kingdom.","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#reached_by/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Country.reached_by/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Country.reached_by(\"machado brazil\")\n  [\"BR\"]\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Country.reached_by(\"United States Kingdom\")\n  [\"US\"]\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Country.reached_by(\"machado de assis\")\n  []","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#reached_by/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Country.resolve_countries/1","doc":"Replaces each country in a changeset with its ISO code: \"UK\" becomes `GB`,\n\"Brasil\" becomes `BR`.\n\nA value naming no country, or more than one, is left as written for\n`validate_countries/1` to refuse by name.\n\nRuns before validation, so rows saying \"UK\" and \"GB\" store the same code,\nand the composite key counts them as one publication rather than two.","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#resolve_countries/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Country.search/2","doc":"The countries `term` finds, named in `locale`.\n\nA country is found by either of its ISO codes, by its name in any language,\nor by one of the other names readers type for it. So \"NL\", \"NLD\",\n\"Netherlands\", \"Países Baixos\" and \"Holanda\" all find the same one. Each\ncountry is as `known/1` gives it.\n\nNames that begin with the term come before names that merely contain it, and\na name matched in full comes before both: one country's name can begin\nanother's, and typing a name in full should not put it behind the longer names\nit starts.","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#search/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Country.validate_code/1","doc":"Adds an `:alpha2` error to the changeset's `code` when the country table has\nno country with that ISO alpha-2 code. Codes are compared as written, so a\nlowercase code is refused.","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#validate_code/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Country.validate_code/1","doc":"iex> import Ecto.Changeset\n  iex> code = &(%RichardBurton.Country{} |> change(code: &1) |> RichardBurton.Country.validate_code())\n  iex> code.(\"BR\").valid?\n  true\n  iex> code.(\"br\").valid?\n  false\n  iex> code.(\"ZZ\").valid?\n  false","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#validate_code/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Country.validate_countries/1","doc":"Validates the `countries` of a changeset, a list of codes or names: there\nmust be at least one, and each must name a country of the table, as\n`code_for/1` reads names. Adds one `:alpha2` error to `countries` that lists\nthe values naming none.","ref":"RichardBurton.Country.html#validate_countries/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Document","doc":"An import document: a named batch of rows being prepared for the database,\nstored so that work on it can continue later.\n\nThe list of documents is shared. A document has no owner and no members,\nbecause everyone preparing the database works on the same data. Anyone who\nmay edit publications may open any document.\n\nA document's content is a Yjs document, and this module never parses it. A\nYjs document is a set of updates that can be applied in any order and more\nthan once, so storing one only needs appending updates and returning them.\nBecause the server does not parse Yjs, the deployment needs no precompiled\nnative dependency. Validating and submitting the rows use the publication\nendpoints, which take plain JSON.\n\nThese terms have specific meanings here:\n\n  * **update** — one change to the content, as Yjs encoded it, stored as\n    opaque bytes.\n  * **compaction** — one update that has the same effect as every update up\n    to a given id, written in their place. It keeps the number of updates a\n    long-lived document has to load small. Once written it is an ordinary\n    update, and nothing marks it or reads it differently.\n  * **archiving** — taking a document off the list without deleting its\n    content.\n  * **cursor** — the position a page of the list starts after: the\n    `updated_at` and id of the last document on the previous page.\n\n`rows` is the row count given with each write. The server cannot count rows\nin content it does not parse, so it stores the count as given and does not\ncheck it.","ref":"RichardBurton.Document.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Document.append/3","doc":"Appends an update to a document and stores `rows`, a count that cannot be\nnegative, as its row count.\n\nIt also sets the document's `updated_at` to now, which moves the document to\nthe top of the list. When `rows` is `nil`, the stored count is kept and the\nupdate is still appended, so a missing count never loses an update.","ref":"RichardBurton.Document.html#append/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Document.archive/1","doc":"Archives a document by setting `archived_at`. The document leaves the list,\nand its updates are kept.\n\nArchiving does not delete anything, because the rows are a record of what was\nprepared. An archived document can still be found by id and can be\nunarchived.","ref":"RichardBurton.Document.html#archive/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Document.compact/3","doc":"Replaces every update of the document up to `through` with the single update\n`merged`.\n\nThe caller builds `merged`, because the server does not parse the content.\n`through` is the id of the last update the caller merged, as returned by\n`updates/2`. Updates with a larger id were appended after that read, so they\nare not in the merge and are kept.\n\nThe old updates are deleted and `merged` is inserted in one transaction, so a\nreader never gets both.","ref":"RichardBurton.Document.html#compact/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Document.create/1","doc":"Creates a document from `attrs`: its `name`, and optionally its `rows`, a\ncount that cannot be negative. Returns `{:ok, document}`, or\n`{:error, changeset}` when a field is invalid.","ref":"RichardBurton.Document.html#create/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Document.find/1","doc":"Returns `{:ok, document}` for the id, or `{:error, :not_found}` when no\ndocument has it, including when the id is not an integer.\n\nIt finds archived documents too, so an archived document stays reachable by\nid.","ref":"RichardBurton.Document.html#find/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Document.page/1","doc":"Returns a page of documents, most recently changed first, as\n`%{entries: documents, more: boolean}`. `more` is true when more documents\nfollow the page.\n\nThe page is not scoped to a person, because the list is shared. It holds the\ndocuments that are not archived, or only the archived ones when\n`archived: true` is given. Documents with the same `updated_at` are ordered\nby id, newest first, so the order is the same on every read.\n\n`limit` sets the page size. It defaults to 50 and is capped at 200, so a\nread never returns the whole table.\n\n`after` is a cursor, and the page starts with the document that follows it.\nA cursor is used instead of an offset because a change moves a document to\nthe top of the list. With an offset, that move would shift every document\nbelow it by one, and a page could repeat or skip a document.","ref":"RichardBurton.Document.html#page/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Document.rename/2","doc":"Renames a document to the `name` in `attrs`. Returns `{:ok, document}`, or\n`{:error, changeset}` when `attrs` has no name, or one that is blank or\nlonger than 200 characters.","ref":"RichardBurton.Document.html#rename/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Document.unarchive/1","doc":"Clears `archived_at`, which puts an archived document back on the list.","ref":"RichardBurton.Document.html#unarchive/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Document.updates/2","doc":"Returns `{updates, through}`: the document's updates in id order, and the id\nof the last one returned.\n\nApplying the updates in any order rebuilds the document's content. `through`\nis the id to pass to `compact/3` later, so that the compaction replaces only\nthe updates that were read and keeps any appended after them.\n\nWith `after: id`, only the updates with a larger id are returned. When there\nare none, `through` is the id that was given. No update is missed this way,\nbecause every write to a document's updates holds the document's row lock\nuntil it commits. Writes to one document therefore commit one at a time, in\nid order, and once an id has been read no update with a smaller id can still\nappear.\n\nEvery update after the given id is returned, compactions included. A\ncompaction deletes the updates it replaces, so any update left beside it is\none it does not include. Returning an update that a reader already has does\nno harm, because Yjs ignores an update it has already applied.","ref":"RichardBurton.Document.html#updates/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Document.Update","doc":"One Yjs update to a document's content, stored as opaque bytes. Updates\nare written in the order of their ids.","ref":"RichardBurton.Document.Update.html"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Email","doc":"An email the platform sends: a message from the contact form to the research\nteam, or a message the platform writes itself, such as a confirmation or an\ninvitation.\n\nAn email without `to` is a contact-form message, and `RichardBurton.Mailer`\nsends it to the research team. An email with `to` is sent to that address.\nThe schema is never stored. It only validates the contact form's fields.","ref":"RichardBurton.Email.html"},{"type":"type","title":"RichardBurton.Email.t/0","doc":"An email, as the contact form fills it in or as the platform writes it.","ref":"RichardBurton.Email.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Email.changeset/2","doc":"Casts the contact form's fields. `name`, `address`, `subject` and `message`\nare required, and `address` must be an email address. `to` is not cast, so\na contact-form message always goes to the research team.","ref":"RichardBurton.Email.html#changeset/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Email.contact/1","doc":"Sends a contact-form message to the research team, then a copy of it to the\nwriter as a confirmation.\n\nReturns `:ok` once the message is sent, whether the confirmation is sent or\nnot. Returns `{:error, changeset}` when a field is invalid, and\n`{:error, :unsent}` when the mailer fails to send the message.","ref":"RichardBurton.Email.html#contact/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Email.deliver/1","doc":"Sends `message` to the address `to`, from the platform itself. Returns `:ok`,\nor `{:error, :unsent}` when the mailer fails to send it.","ref":"RichardBurton.Email.html#deliver/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.FlatPublication","doc":"A publication in its flat shape: the title, year, countries, publishers,\ntranslators (`authors`), original title, original authors, sources and\nnotes side by side. Each list of countries, publishers, authors or sources\nholds names, country codes or source texts. The notes are rich text, as\n`RichardBurton.RichText` describes it, or nil.\n\nThe schema reads the `flat_publications` materialized view, which holds one\nrow for each publication that is not deleted, and which\n`RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Refresher` rebuilds after each write. It\nalso validates a publication in this shape, without writing it.\n\nThe view does not hold the notes, so `notes` is a virtual field. A\npublication read from the view has nil notes, whatever notes it has, unless\nthe query that read it selected them from the `publications` table.\n\nA flat publication is encoded as JSON with its id, its writable fields,\n`excerpts` and `marked`, as `json_fields/1` returns them. A publication\nloaded from the database is encoded without `notes` when they are nil,\nbecause a nil there may only mean that the query did not read them. Any\nother publication, such as one that `RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.flatten/1`\nreturns, is encoded with its notes, nil included.","ref":"RichardBurton.FlatPublication.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.FlatPublication.json_fields/1","doc":"Returns the fields of `publication` that its JSON holds, as a map, as the\nmodule doc describes.","ref":"RichardBurton.FlatPublication.html#json_fields/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.FlatPublication.validate/2","doc":"Validates `attrs` as `validate_all/2` validates each entry of a list.","ref":"RichardBurton.FlatPublication.html#validate/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.FlatPublication.validate_all/2","doc":"Validates each of `attrs_list` without writing anything, and returns the\nresults in order.\n\nA result is `{:error, errors}` when the attrs are invalid, `{:error,\n:conflict}` when a stored publication that is not deleted has their\ncomposite key, and `:ok` otherwise. The publication with the id\n`exclude_id` is left out of the key check, so an edit does not count against\nitself. The keys are looked up with one query for the whole list.\n\nThe lookup reads the publications table rather than the materialized view\nbehind this schema, so it also finds a publication inserted since the view\nwas last refreshed. Each entry is checked against the stored publications\nonly, not against the other entries. `Publication.Duplicates.resemblances/1`\nreports an entry that repeats an earlier one.","ref":"RichardBurton.FlatPublication.html#validate_all/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Identity","doc":"The composite keys that stop an original book, a translated book or a\npublication from being stored twice. The database computes and enforces\nthem, and this module is how the application asks it.\n\nA *fingerprint* is a hash of a set of names. The keys are:\n\n  * an original book: its title, and the fingerprint of its authors' names;\n  * a translated book: the original book it translates, and the fingerprint\n    of its translators' names;\n  * a publication that is not deleted: its title, year and translated book,\n    and the fingerprints of its publishers' names and its country codes.\n\nThe database computes a fingerprint with `rb_set_fingerprint`, from the names\nsorted, so the same names in another order give the same fingerprint. Each\nstored fingerprint is kept up to date by triggers when a link is added or\nremoved, or a linked name changes (see the migration\n`ComputeFingerprintsInTheDatabase`). The database also has functions that\nfind the stored row with a key (see the migration\n`LookUpCompositeKeysInTheDatabase`), and `books_with_keys/1` and\n`publications_with_keys/2` call them for many keys at once.\n\nThe keys are checked when a transaction commits, because a row's links are\nwritten one by one after the row, and until the last one is written the row\ncan match another row's key. `settle/0` checks them earlier, so that a write\ncan report a conflict as its own result.","ref":"RichardBurton.Identity.html"},{"type":"type","title":"RichardBurton.Identity.key_fields/0","doc":"The fields of a flat publication that its composite key is built from. Names\nand country codes are lists, in any order. Other fields are allowed and\nignored.","ref":"RichardBurton.Identity.html#t:key_fields/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Identity.books_with_keys/1","doc":"Returns, for each `{title, authors, translators}` in `keys`, the ids of the\nstored original book with that title and those authors, and of its stored\ntranslated book by those translators, as a pair. Either id is nil when there\nis no such book, and the translated book's is nil whenever the original\nbook's is. Names are lists in any order. The pairs come in the order of\n`keys`, from one query.","ref":"RichardBurton.Identity.html#books_with_keys/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Identity.publications_in_conflict/1","doc":"Returns the ids among `ids` of the publications that have the same key as\nanother publication that is not deleted, or whose translated or original\nbook has the same key as another book.\n\nOf two publications in `ids` with the same key, only the one with the higher\nid is returned. A publication outside `ids` counts whatever its id.","ref":"RichardBurton.Identity.html#publications_in_conflict/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Identity.publications_with_keys/2","doc":"Returns, for each of `publications`, the id of a stored publication that is\nnot deleted and has its key, or nil when there is none. The ids come in the\norder of `publications`, from one query. The publication with the id\n`excluded` is left out, so an edit does not match the publication it edits.\n\nEach of `publications` holds the fields in `t:key_fields/0`, where `:authors`\nare the translators.","ref":"RichardBurton.Identity.html#publications_with_keys/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Identity.settle/0","doc":"Checks the composite keys now, inside the current transaction, with one call\nto the database function `rb_settle` (see the migration\n`CheckCompositeKeysInOneCall`).\n\nReturns `:ok`, or `{:error, :conflict}` when a row has the same key as\nanother. After a conflict, only the check is rolled back, so the transaction\ncan still run queries, for instance to find the rows in conflict. The keys\nare deferred again afterwards, so the next write in the same transaction can\npass through states that match another key, as the first could.","ref":"RichardBurton.Identity.html#settle/0"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Invitation","doc":"A role held for an email address until whoever owns it signs in.\n\nAn admin cannot grant a role to someone who has never signed in, because the\nidentity provider mints the subject id only on a first sign-in. So the role\nwaits on the address instead, and the first sign-in with that address\nredeems it. The provider vouching for the address is the confirmation, so no\nseparate confirmation link is sent. `RichardBurton.Auth.Claims` refuses a\ntoken that does not carry a verified email, which is what this rests on.\n\nSomeone who already has an account is given the role outright, and no\ninvitation is recorded.\n\nAn invitation is **pending** until it is redeemed, and **accepted** after.\nAn address has at most one pending invitation.","ref":"RichardBurton.Invitation.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Invitation.admit/2","doc":"Admits a signed-in identity when it is one this platform knows.\n\nAn account is made only for someone who was invited. Anyone may hold a Google\naccount, so a sign-in that created one regardless would let whoever tries\ndecide who has an account, and revoking an account would mean nothing, since\nsigning in again would make another. The new account takes up the pending\ninvitation in the same transaction, so it starts with the offered role.\n\nSomeone who already has an account is admitted on the strength of it. If a\npending invitation is also waiting for their address, they take it up, and\ntheir role is raised to the offered one when the offered one is higher. An\ninvitation never lowers a role.\n\nReturns `{:ok, user}`, `:not_invited` for someone with neither an account\nnor a pending invitation, or `{:error, changeset}` when the account cannot\nbe made.","ref":"RichardBurton.Invitation.html#admit/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Invitation.all/0","doc":"Every invitation, newest first, pending and accepted.","ref":"RichardBurton.Invitation.html#all/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Invitation.cancel/1","doc":"Withdraws a pending invitation by deleting it. Returns `{:ok, invitation}`,\nor `{:error, :accepted}` for an invitation already accepted.","ref":"RichardBurton.Invitation.html#cancel/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Invitation.changeset/2","doc":"Casts a new invitation's `email`, `role` and `invited_by_id`. The email,\ntrimmed, must be an email address, and the role is required.","ref":"RichardBurton.Invitation.html#changeset/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Invitation.get/1","doc":"Returns the invitation with `id`, or nil.","ref":"RichardBurton.Invitation.html#get/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Invitation.invite/2","doc":"Offers the role in `attrs[\"role\"]` to the address in `attrs[\"email\"]`, on\nbehalf of `invited_by`, and mails the offer.\n\nSomeone who already has an account gets the role now, and no invitation is\nrecorded. Returns `{:ok, {:granted, user}}`, or the errors of\n`RichardBurton.User.set_role/3`.\n\nOtherwise it records a pending invitation and mails it. Returns\n`{:ok, {:invited, invitation}}`, or `{:ok, {:unsent, invitation}}` when the\nmail could not be sent. The invitation is recorded first, so a failure to\nsend does not lose it, and it can be sent again with `resend/1`.\n\nReturns `{:error, :invalid_role}` when the role is missing or is not a role,\n`{:error, :pending}` when the address already has a pending invitation, and\n`{:error, changeset}` when the address is not one.","ref":"RichardBurton.Invitation.html#invite/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Invitation.resend/1","doc":"Mails a pending invitation again. Returns `{:ok, invitation}`,\n`{:error, :unsent}` when the mail could not be sent, or `{:error, :accepted}`\nfor an invitation already accepted.","ref":"RichardBurton.Invitation.html#resend/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Mailer","doc":"Sends a `RichardBurton.Email` through Swoosh.\n\nThe Swoosh adapter is the `:mailer_adapter` application setting, and SMTP\nwhen it is unset. The SMTP settings are read from the environment on every\nsend.","ref":"RichardBurton.Mailer.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Mailer.deliver/2","doc":"Delivers an email.\n\nIf the email is delivered it returns an `{:ok, result}` tuple. If it fails,\nreturns an `{:error, error}` tuple.","ref":"RichardBurton.Mailer.html#deliver/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Mailer.deliver!/2","doc":"Delivers an email, raises on error.\n\nIf the email is delivered, it returns the result. If it fails, it raises\na `DeliveryError`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Mailer.html#deliver!/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Mailer.deliver_many/2","doc":"Delivers a list of emails.\n\nIt accepts a list of `%Swoosh.Email{}` as its first parameter.","ref":"RichardBurton.Mailer.html#deliver_many/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Mailer.send/1","doc":"Sends `email`, and returns what the adapter returns: `{:ok, result}` or\n`{:error, reason}`.\n\nAn adapter that raises, as Swoosh's SMTP adapter does when `SMTP_HOST` is\nunset, gives `{:error, exception}`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Mailer.html#send/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Name","doc":"Functions shared by the records that are found by a name, such as\n`RichardBurton.Author`, `RichardBurton.Publisher` and the titles of\n`RichardBurton.OriginalBook`.\n\nA name is searched in two passes. The **prefix** pass finds the names that\nstart with the term, ignoring case. When it finds none, the **fuzzy** pass\nfinds the names that share enough trigrams with the term to be a misspelling\nof it.","ref":"RichardBurton.Name.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Name.flatten/1","doc":"Flattens records or maps with a name to the names they are, as `get_name/1`\nreads each one. A value that is not a list is returned unchanged.","ref":"RichardBurton.Name.html#flatten/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Name.flatten/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Name.flatten([%{\"name\" => \"Noonday Press\"}, %{\"name\" => \"Penguin\"}])\n  [\"Noonday Press\", \"Penguin\"]\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Name.flatten(\"Noonday Press\")\n  \"Noonday Press\"","ref":"RichardBurton.Name.html#flatten/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Name.get_name/1","doc":"Returns the name of a record with a `name` field or of a map with a `:name`\nor `\"name\"` key, and nil for any other map. Any other value, such as a\nstring, is returned unchanged, so a name that is not a string reaches the\nchangeset and is refused there.","ref":"RichardBurton.Name.html#get_name/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Name.nest/1","doc":"Nests a flat publication's list of names into the maps a schema with a\n`name` field casts. A value that is not a list is returned unchanged, for the\nchangeset to refuse.\n\nSplitting a written-down string into names is done by the CSV codec, before\na value reaches here.","ref":"RichardBurton.Name.html#nest/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Name.nest/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Name.nest([\"Machado de Assis\", \"Clarice Lispector\"])\n  [%{\"name\" => \"Machado de Assis\"}, %{\"name\" => \"Clarice Lispector\"}]\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Name.nest(\"Machado de Assis\")\n  \"Machado de Assis\"","ref":"RichardBurton.Name.html#nest/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Name.search/2","doc":"Returns the rows of `schema` whose `name` starts with `term`, or, when none\ndoes, the rows whose `name` resembles it.","ref":"RichardBurton.Name.html#search/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Name.search/3","doc":"Returns the rows of `schema` whose `name` matches `term` in one pass:\n`:prefix` or `:fuzzy`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Name.html#search/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Name.similar_to/3","doc":"Returns a condition that holds when `field` of the named binding `binding`\nis similar enough to `term` to be a misspelling of it.","ref":"RichardBurton.Name.html#similar_to/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Name.starts_with/3","doc":"Returns a condition that holds when `field` of the named binding `binding`\nstarts with `term`, ignoring case.","ref":"RichardBurton.Name.html#starts_with/3"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook","doc":"An original book: a title and the authors who wrote it.\n\nTwo original books with the same title and the same authors are the same\nbook, and the database keeps only one (see `RichardBurton.Identity`).\n`authors_fingerprint` is the fingerprint of the authors' names, which the\ndatabase writes, so the schema never writes it.","ref":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.all/0","doc":"Returns every original book, with its authors.","ref":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.html#all/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.flatten/1","doc":"Returns a map of a book's title and its authors' names. The authors must be\nloaded.","ref":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.html#flatten/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.OriginalBook.flatten/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.OriginalBook.flatten(%RichardBurton.OriginalBook{\n  ...>   title: \"Dom Casmurro\",\n  ...>   authors: [%RichardBurton.Author{name: \"Machado de Assis\"}]\n  ...> })\n  %{title: \"Dom Casmurro\", authors: [\"Machado de Assis\"]}","ref":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.html#flatten/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.search/1","doc":"Returns the original books whose title or one of whose authors' names\nbegins with `term`, as `search/2` with `:prefix` does. When none does, it\nreturns the ones whose title or author's name resembles `term`, as\n`search/2` with `:fuzzy` does.","ref":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.html#search/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.search/2","doc":"Returns the original books whose title or one of whose authors' names\nbegins with `term`, with `:prefix`, or resembles it, with `:fuzzy`, ordered\nby title, with their authors.","ref":"RichardBurton.OriginalBook.html#search/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Publication","doc":"A publication: one translated edition of an original book, and the write paths\nthat change one.\n\nIdentity is a composite key: the title, the year, the translated book, and\nthe fingerprints of the countries and publishers. The database computes and\nenforces it (see `RichardBurton.Identity`), so the same edition cannot be\nstored twice. The near-duplicates that key cannot catch are\n`Publication.Duplicates`.\n\nFive words carry specific meanings here:\n\n  * **tombstone** — a soft-deleted row. `delete/2` sets `deleted_at` rather\n    than removing anything, so the row, its sources and its history are kept\n    and `restore/3` can bring it back. Every read leaves tombstones out, and\n    the composite-key index is partial, so a tombstone never blocks\n    importing the same publication again.\n  * **winner** and **losers** — in a merge, the publication that is kept and\n    the publications merged into it. The winner keeps its own fields. The\n    losers add their countries, publishers, sources and notes to it, and are\n    then tombstoned. A merge given notes of its own sets the winner's notes\n    to them instead of adding the losers' (see `merge/4`).\n  * **absorbed** — the state of a loser after the merge, as\n    `Publication.History.absorbed/1` defines it. It is not in the trash, and\n    `restore/3` refuses it with `{:error, :absorbed}`. Undoing the merge\n    brings it back.\n  * **compensate** — to apply the action that `undo/3` uses to reverse a\n    recorded entry. The compensating action is recorded as a new entry, which\n    can be undone in turn.\n\nA publication's `notes` are rich text about it, as `RichardBurton.RichText`\ndescribes it, or nil when it has none. Blank notes are stored as nil. The\nnotes are not part of the composite key, and the search does not read them.\n\nA publication's countries, publishers, translators and original authors are\nsets. The link tables keep no order, and a preload reads each set sorted, by\ncode for countries and by name for the others, as the search index lists\nthem. A history snapshot lists them in that order too, so two snapshots\ndiffer only when a set changed.\n\nEvery mutation records an entry in `Publication.History` inside the same\ntransaction, and signals `Publication.Index.Refresher` once per operation.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html"},{"type":"type","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.t/0","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.all/0","doc":"Returns every publication row, tombstones included, ordered by id, which is\nthe order they were inserted. Each row comes with its countries, publishers,\nsources and translated book preloaded.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#all/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.all_deleted/0","doc":"Returns the publications someone deleted, preloaded, the most recently\ndeleted first. The absorbed publications are left out: nobody deleted them,\nand only undoing the merge brings them back.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#all_deleted/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.delete/2","doc":"Tombstones the publication with the id `id`, and records a `deleted` history\nentry by `actor`, in one transaction. Every read leaves the publication out\nafterwards, and `restore/3` can bring it back.\n\nReturns `{:ok, publication}`, or `{:error, :not_found}` when there is no\nsuch publication that is not deleted.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#delete/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.find/1","doc":"Returns the publication with the id `id`, preloaded, or `nil` when there is\nnone or it is deleted.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#find/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.insert/2","doc":"Inserts the publication `attrs` describes, and records it with a `created`\nhistory entry by `actor`, in one transaction.\n\nReturns `{:ok, publication}`, preloaded. Returns `{:error, errors}` when\n`attrs` is invalid, and `{:error, :conflict}` when a stored publication has\nthe same composite key. It is `insert_all/2` with a batch of one.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#insert/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.insert_all/2","doc":"Inserts the publications `attrs_list` describes in one transaction, all or\nnone, records each with a `created` history entry by `actor`, and signals\nthe refresher once. See `Publication.Import.insert_all/2` for what it\nreturns.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#insert_all/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.merge/4","doc":"Merges the publications with the ids `loser_ids` into the one with the id\n`winner_id`, and records the merge with one `merged` entry by `actor` on the\nwinner, in one transaction. `actor` must be a string and `opts` a keyword\nlist, so a call that passes options passes the actor before them.\n\nThe winner keeps its title, year, translated book and original book. It\ngains the countries and publishers of the losers, and their sources after\nits own, leaving out a source it already holds. The losers are then\ntombstoned, and their distinctions from each other and from the winner are\ndeleted (see `Publication.Duplicates.forget/1`). The entry names the losers, so `undo/3`\ncan undo the whole merge.\n\nThe merged notes are the value of the `:notes` option when `opts` holds it,\ncast and validated as the notes of an update are, so nil or a blank value\nleaves the winner without notes. Without that option, the merged notes are\nthe winner's notes followed by the blocks of each loser's notes, in the\norder of `loser_ids`. A loser's notes are left out when they are nil,\nblank, or equal to notes already included. When the winner has no notes,\nthe merged notes are the losers'.\n\nReturns `{:ok, winner}`, preloaded. Returns `{:error, :conflict}` when the\nmerged publication would have the composite key of another publication,\n`{:error, :not_found}` when one of the publications is missing or deleted,\n`{:error, :self}` when `loser_ids` holds `winner_id`,\n`{:error, :no_losers}` when `loser_ids` is empty, and `{:error, errors}`\nwhen the merged publication is invalid, such as when the merged notes are\ntoo long, or the `:notes` value is not rich text. Nothing is written when\nit returns an error.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#merge/4"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.preload/1","doc":"Preloads the countries, publishers, sources and translated book of a\npublication or a list of them, with the translated book's authors and\noriginal book, and the original book's authors.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#preload/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.restore/3","doc":"Brings back the deleted publication with the id `id`, and records a\n`restored` history entry by `actor`, in one transaction. When `changes` are\ngiven, they are applied to the publication first, in the same transaction,\nand recorded as an update before the restore.\n\nReturns `{:ok, publication}`. Returns `{:error, :not_found}` when there is no\nsuch deleted publication, and `{:error, :absorbed}` when the publication is\nabsorbed, which only undoing the merge brings back.\n\nWhen a publication that is not deleted has the same composite key, which\nhappens when the same publication was imported again or a name was\ncorrected to match, the result is `{:error, {:conflict, twin}}`, where\n`twin` is that publication. Nothing is written then, not even the changes.\nChanges that do not validate return their errors and write nothing either.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#restore/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.undo/3","doc":"Undoes the entry with the version `version` in the history of the\npublication with the id `id`, by applying the action that compensates it.\nThe compensating action is recorded as a new entry by `actor`.\n\nReturns the result of that action. Returns `{:error, :not_found}` when there\nis no such entry, and `{:error, :conflict}` when the entry is not undoable\n(see `Publication.History.undoable?/3`).\n\nAn un-merge is undone by merging the same publications again, with the\nnotes that the merge it undid left on the winner.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#undo/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.update/3","doc":"Applies `attrs` to the publication with the id `id` that is not deleted,\nand records the change with an `updated` history entry by `actor`, in one\ntransaction. A save that changes nothing records no entry.\n\nReturns `{:ok, publication}`, preloaded. Returns `{:error, :not_found}` when\nthere is no such publication, `{:error, errors}` when `attrs` is invalid,\nand `{:error, :conflict}` when another publication has the composite key the\nchange would give it.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#update/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.with_ids/1","doc":"Returns the publications with the ids `ids`, deleted ones included,\npreloaded, in the order of `ids`. Every id must be a stored publication's.\n\nThe countries, publishers, translated book, original book and both lists of\nauthors are read in one query that joins them, sorted the way the\nassociations' `preload_order` sorts them. The sources are read in a second\nquery, so that many sources do not multiply the rows of the first.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.html#with_ids/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Chart","doc":"Describes a chart of publications, and counts what the chart shows.\n\nA chart counts **items** in **groups**. An item is what `count` names, and a\ngroup is what `by` names. A group counts each item once, however many of its\npublications hold it. A chart can also split each group into **series**, or\ninto **steps** of time. `parse/1` reads a chart from request parameters, and\n`draw/2` counts it.\n\nA chart has these fields:\n\n  * `count` — the items: `:publications`, `:works`, `:translations`,\n    `:original_authors`, `:translators` or `:publishers`. Works\n    and translations are those `RichardBurton.Publication.Insights` defines.\n  * `by` — the groups: `:year`, `:decade`, `:kind`, `:country`,\n    `:original_author`, `:translator`, `:publisher`, `:work`, or `:pair`,\n    which is an original author and a translator together. A publication is\n    in one year, decade, kind and work, and in one group for each country,\n    name or pair it holds. A decade is named by its first year.\n  * `split` — the series: `nil` for none, `:kind`, or `:country`. A\n    publication is in one series. With `:country`, the series are the\n    leading countries and one more for every other country, and a\n    publication is in the first leading country it holds. Items other than\n    publications can be in several series of a group, so the counts of a\n    group's series can add up to more than its total.\n  * `over` — the steps: `nil` for none, `:year` or `:decade`. It needs a `by`\n    that is not a year or a decade, and no `split`.\n  * `first` — when true, each item is counted at its first appearance only:\n    in the group and step of its earliest publication in the whole index, by\n    year and then by id, and in that publication's series. Within a group of\n    names, works, pairs or countries, an item's earliest publication is the\n    earliest in that group. Only the items the counted publications hold are\n    counted, even when their first appearance is outside them. It needs `by`\n    or `over` to be a year or a decade.\n  * `top` — how many groups to keep, or `nil` to keep them all.\n  * `least` — the fewest items a group needs to be kept, or `nil` to keep\n    groups of any size.\n\nGroups by year or decade are listed by time, and groups by kind in the order\nof `RichardBurton.Publication.Kind.all/0`. They cannot take `top` or `least`.\nEvery other group is ranked, with the most items first. Groups with as many\nitems are sorted by their number of publications, most first, and then by\ntheir names.\n\nTerms used here:\n\n  * **kind** — whether a publication is a first translation, a\n    retranslation or a reissue, as `RichardBurton.Publication.Kind` defines\n    them. Kinds are judged over the whole index, so a search does not turn a\n    reissue into a first translation.\n  * **leading countries** — the two countries with the most publications\n    among those counted. Countries with the same count are sorted by code.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Chart.html"},{"type":"type","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Chart.t/0","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Chart.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Chart.draw/2","doc":"Counts `chart` over the publications `base` selects, a query of\n`FlatPublication`.\n\nReturns a map with these keys:\n\n  * `series` — the series, in order. They are `[nil]` without a split, the\n    kinds with `split: :kind`, and the codes of the leading countries\n    followed by `nil` for every other country with `split: :country`.\n  * `groups` — the groups, in the order the module doc gives. Groups by year\n    or decade run from the earliest to the latest, including those with no\n    items, and groups by kind include all three kinds. Each group has `key`,\n    `total`, the number of distinct items in the group, and `counts`, the\n    number in each series, in the order of `series`. With `over`, it also\n    has `steps`.\n  * `hidden` — the number of ranked groups that `top` leaves out.\n\nA group's `key` is a year or a decade as an integer, a kind, a country code\nor a name as a string, `%{title: title, authors: authors}` for a work, and\n`%{author: author, translator: translator}` for a pair.\n\nEach step has `at`, its year or decade, `total`, the number of distinct items\nin the group in that step, and `items`, the names of those items. An item's\nnames are a publication's title, a work's original title, a translation's\ntranslators, or the one name or country code it is. Items with the same\nnames are listed once. A group's steps are only those with items, earliest\nfirst.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Chart.html#draw/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Chart.nothing/1","doc":"Returns what `draw/2` returns for `chart` when no publication is counted,\nwithout reading the database.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Chart.html#nothing/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Chart.parse/1","doc":"Reads a chart from request parameters, with string keys. A missing field\ntakes its default, and parameters that are not fields are ignored.\n\nReturns `{:ok, chart}`, or `{:error, errors}` with `:invalid` for each field\nwhose value is not one of its values, or does not go with the other fields.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Chart.html#parse/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec","doc":"Converts publications between their flat and nested shapes, and reads and\nwrites them as CSV.\n\nThe **flat** shape is the one `RichardBurton.FlatPublication` has: `title`,\n`year`, `countries`, `publishers`, `authors`, `original_title`,\n`original_authors`, `sources` and `notes`, side by side. Each list of\ncountries, publishers, authors or sources holds names, codes or source\ntexts, and the notes are rich text (see `RichardBurton.RichText`).\n\nThe **nested** shape is the one `RichardBurton.Publication.changeset/2`\ncasts. Its list fields hold child maps, such as `%{\"name\" => name}`, and the\ntranslators, the original title and the original authors are inside the\ntranslated book:\n\n    %{\n      \"title\" => title,\n      \"year\" => year,\n      \"notes\" => blocks,\n      \"countries\" => [%{\"code\" => code}],\n      \"publishers\" => [%{\"name\" => name}],\n      \"sources\" => [%{\"content\" => text, \"position\" => 0}],\n      \"translated_book\" => %{\n        \"authors\" => [%{\"name\" => name}],\n        \"original_book\" => %{\"title\" => title, \"authors\" => [%{\"name\" => name}]}\n      }\n    }","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.flatten/1","doc":"Flattens a nested publication into the flat shape, the inverse of `nest/1`.\nTakes a `Publication` struct, a nested map with atom or string keys, a list,\nor a `%{publication:, errors:}` pair.\n\nA map comes back with string keys. Its list fields hold the names or codes of\ntheir child maps. The translated book's `authors`, and its original book's\n`title` and `authors`, come back as `authors`, `original_title` and\n`original_authors`. Any other field of the translated book or the original\nbook is left out. When `translated_book` is not a map, such as the error\n`:required`, it is kept under its own key. Every other entry keeps its key and\nvalue, nested maps included.\n\nA `Publication` struct comes back as a `FlatPublication` struct with the same\nid. A pair comes back with its publication flattened, and with its errors\nas `flatten_errors/1` returns them.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.html#flatten/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.flatten/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.flatten(%{\n  ...>   \"title\" => \"Dom Casmurro\",\n  ...>   \"translated_book\" => %{\n  ...>     \"authors\" => [%{\"name\" => \"Helen Caldwell\"}],\n  ...>     \"original_book\" => %{\n  ...>       \"title\" => \"Dom Casmurro\",\n  ...>       \"authors\" => [%{\"name\" => \"Machado de Assis\"}]\n  ...>     }\n  ...>   }\n  ...> })\n  %{\"authors\" => [\"Helen Caldwell\"], \"original_authors\" => [\"Machado de Assis\"], \"original_title\" => \"Dom Casmurro\", \"title\" => \"Dom Casmurro\"}\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.flatten(%{\n  ...>   translated_book: %{id: 7, original_book: %{title: \"Iracema\"}}\n  ...> })\n  %{\"original_title\" => \"Iracema\"}","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.html#flatten/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.flatten_errors/1","doc":"Flattens the errors of a nested publication, as\n`RichardBurton.Validation.get_errors/1` returns them, into errors by flat\nfield, the shape `RichardBurton.FlatPublication.validate/2` reports. Nil and\nan atom, such as `:conflict`, are returned as they are.\n\nA map comes back with string keys, and with the errors of the translated\nbook's fields under the flat names `flatten/1` gives them. The errors of a\nlist of child maps, such as the countries, become one code: the code of the\nfirst child that has an error.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.html#flatten_errors/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.flatten_errors/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.flatten_errors(%{\n    ...>   countries: [%{}, %{code: :alpha2}],\n    ...>   translated_book: %{original_book: %{title: :required}}\n    ...> })\n    %{\"countries\" => :alpha2, \"original_title\" => :required}","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.html#flatten_errors/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.from_csv/1","doc":"Reads a CSV of publications: one row each, columns named by a header.\n\nThe header decides which column is which, so they may be written in any order\nand a column the file does not carry reads as empty. A name the table does not\nknow is ignored, which is what lets a spreadsheet keep a column of its own.\n\nA `notes` cell holds plain text, which may run over several lines inside\nquotes, and reads as the rich text `RichardBurton.RichText.from_plain_text/1`\nreturns for it: a paragraph for each part of the text between blank lines,\nwith a link for each web address or email address in it. A blank or\nmissing `notes` cell reads as nil.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.html#from_csv/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.nest/1","doc":"Nests a flat publication into the nested shape. Takes a `FlatPublication`\nstruct, a flat map with atom or string keys, or a list of either.\n\nA map comes back with string keys. Its list fields hold child maps, and its\n`authors`, `original_title` and `original_authors` are under\n`translated_book`. Only the fields the map holds are written: a map without\n`authors` gets a translated book without `\"authors\"`, and a map without any\nof the three gets no translated book. Every other entry keeps its key and\nvalue.\n\nA `FlatPublication` comes back as the `Publication` struct the nested shape\ncasts to.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.html#nest/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.nest/1","doc":"iex> nested =\n  ...>   RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.nest(%{\n  ...>     \"title\" => \"Dom Casmurro\",\n  ...>     \"authors\" => [\"Helen Caldwell\"],\n  ...>     \"original_title\" => \"Dom Casmurro\",\n  ...>     \"original_authors\" => [\"Machado de Assis\"]\n  ...>   })\n  iex> nested[\"translated_book\"][\"authors\"]\n  [%{\"name\" => \"Helen Caldwell\"}]\n  iex> nested[\"translated_book\"][\"original_book\"][\"title\"]\n  \"Dom Casmurro\"\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.nest(%{original_title: \"Iracema\"})\n  %{\"translated_book\" => %{\"original_book\" => %{\"title\" => \"Iracema\"}}}","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.html#nest/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.to_csv/1","doc":"Writes publications as a CSV with a header, in the shape `from_csv/1` reads.\n\nThe columns come in this order: `title`, `year`, `countries`, `publishers`,\n`authors`, `original_title`, `original_authors`, `sources` and `notes`.\nThe file has a column for each of them that the first publication holds, so\nevery publication must hold the same fields. The notes are written as the\nplain text `RichardBurton.RichText.to_plain_text/1` returns for them.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.html#to_csv/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates","doc":"Finds publications that are likely the same record entered twice.\n\nThe composite key already blocks exact duplicates, so what remains are the\nnear-matches it cannot catch: a typo, a dropped accent, `St.` for `Saint`, a\ntranslator entered as \"R. Burton\" once and \"Richard Burton\" the next time.\n\nSix words carry specific meanings here:\n\n  * **candidate pair** — two publications similar enough to be worth review.\n  * **cluster** — a connected component of the candidate graph. If A matches B\n    and B matches C, all three form one cluster, because merging them is one\n    operation.\n  * **distinction** — a stored record that two publications are not the same,\n    so the review stops offering them. See `Distinction`.\n  * **ruled apart** — the state of a pair that has a distinction.\n  * **row** — a publication being imported that is not stored yet. It has no\n    id, so `resemblances/1` refers to it by its position in the list it is\n    given.\n  * **repeat** — a row with the same composite key as an earlier row in the\n    same list: the same title, year and original title, and the same names\n    in each list, in any order. The database would refuse to store both\n    rows, so a repeat is an error. A resemblance is not an error.\n\nSimilarity is trigram distance, the measure the author lookup also uses, over\nthe fields a duplicate would agree on. A pair is a candidate when the\ntranslators are similar, either the titles are or the original books are, and\nthe two agree on the year, the countries and the publishers.\n\nTwo publications agree on one of those fields when either of them has no value\nfor it. Otherwise they agree on the year when the years are equal, on the\ncountries when they share a country, and on the publishers when the\npublishers are similar or one side's contain the other's words, as \"Alfred A.\nKnopf\" contains \"Knopf\". So another edition of a book, with its own year,\ncountry or publisher, is not proposed. A record that leaves those fields\nempty can still be.\n\nThe translators are the required half because this is a database of\ntranslations: two people translating one book is the subject matter, not an\nerror. \"Dom Casmurro\" translated by Helen Caldwell and by John Gledson share a\ntitle and an original book and are two distinct publications.\n\nWhen one side leaves the year, countries or publishers out, nothing can tell\ntwo editions of one book from two records of one edition, so the rule proposes\nand a reviewer decides. Distinctions persist that decision, which is what\nmakes the queue converge.\n\n`clusters/0` and `resemblances/1` use the same similarity rule. `clusters/0`\ncompares stored records with each other. `resemblances/1` compares rows with\nthe stored records and with each other.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.clusters/0","doc":"Returns the clusters worth reviewing, the likeliest first. Each cluster has\n`publications`, its flat publications ordered by title and then id, and\n`score`, the highest title similarity among its candidate pairs.\n\nPairs ruled apart are not candidate pairs, so ruling one apart can split a\ncluster in two. The clusters are found with a number of queries that does\nnot depend on their number.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.html#clusters/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.forget/1","doc":"Deletes the distinctions among these publications and returns how many it\ndeleted. It records no history entry, so it suits a change that records its\nown, such as a merge, which answers the question the distinctions answered.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.html#forget/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.reconsider/2","doc":"Deletes the distinctions among these publications, returning the pair or\ncluster to the review queue.\n\nEvery pair among the ids is deleted, mirroring how `rule_apart/2` records\nthem: the decision covered the cluster, so reversing it does too.\n\nWhen a distinction is deleted, each of the publications gets a\n`\"reconsidered\"` history entry under `actor`, which names the others.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.html#reconsider/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.resemblances/1","doc":"Returns what each row resembles, among the stored records and the other rows\nin the list, and which earlier row it repeats.\n\nThe result has one entry for each row that resembles something or is a\nrepeat, in position order. An entry holds `position`, the row's index in\n`rows`; `stored`, the flat publications it resembles; `others`, the positions\nof the other rows it resembles; and `repeats`, the position of the first row\nwith the same composite key when the row is a repeat, or nil. A row that\nresembles nothing and is not a repeat has no entry.\n\nRows are string-keyed flat publications, the same shape validation takes.\nThis function writes nothing. It does not check or record distinctions,\nbecause a distinction links two stored records and a row is not stored.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.html#resemblances/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.rule_apart/2","doc":"Stores a distinction for every pair among the publications with the ids\n`ids`, so `clusters/0` no longer offers them, and returns `{:ok, count}`,\nthe number of distinctions it stored. A pair ruled apart already is kept as\nit was. Returns `{:error, :not_enough}` when `ids` holds fewer than two ids.\n\nWhen it stores any distinction, each of the publications gets a\n`\"distinguished\"` history entry by `actor`, which names the others (see\n`RichardBurton.Publication.History`).","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.html#rule_apart/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.ruled_apart/0","doc":"Returns the pairs someone ruled apart, the newest first. Each has\n`publications`, the two flat publications ordered by title and then id,\n`actor`, who ruled them apart, and `timestamp`, when.\n\nA pair is left out when either publication is no longer in the index,\nbecause a merge or a deletion took it out. The pairs are read with two\nqueries, whatever their number.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.html#ruled_apart/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.threshold/0","doc":"Returns the trigram similarity, between 0 and 1, above which two values are\nalike. It is read at run time from the `:duplicate_threshold` setting, so it\ncan be tuned without a change to the code.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.html#threshold/0"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.Distinction","doc":"One remembered \"these two are not the same\". Stored for the pair, lower id\nfirst, so the answer is the same however it is asked.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates.Distinction.html"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History","doc":"The append-only log of changes to publications. Each row records one change\nto one publication: the action, a flat snapshot of the publication after it,\nthe actor, and a version that numbers the publication's entries in order.\n\nThe database keeps the log append-only: a trigger rejects every UPDATE and\nDELETE on the table (see the migration `SoftDeletePublications`). Every\nchange goes through the `RichardBurton.Publication` functions, or through\n`RichardBurton.Vocabulary.rename/5` for a change of name, and each records\nits entries inside the transaction that makes the change. So a change and its\nentries commit or roll back together.\n\nTerms used here:\n\n  * **stream** — the entries of one publication, newest first.\n  * **head** — the latest entry of a stream that is not a decision. See\n    `head/1`.\n  * **others** — the other publications an entry is about, each with its\n    snapshot when the entry was recorded. The entry's action says how they\n    are involved: a merge absorbed them, an un-merge gave them back, a\n    decision is about them, or the same rename changed them. A rename's\n    entries are stored without them, and `of/1` and `all/0` read them from\n    the rename's other entries. An entry of any other action has none.\n  * **absorbed** — the state of a publication held inside another by a\n    merge. A `\"merged\"` entry is on the publication that survived the merge,\n    and names the publications it absorbed in its `others`. An `\"unmerged\"`\n    entry names the ones it gave back. See `absorbed/1`.\n  * **decision** — a duplicate reviewer's answer about publications that look\n    alike, recorded by `RichardBurton.Publication.Duplicates`. A\n    `\"distinguished\"` entry records that the publications are not the same\n    record twice, and a `\"reconsidered\"` entry that the answer was taken\n    back. Each publication the decision is about gets an entry, which names\n    the other publications in its `others`. A decision changes no field, so\n    its entry has no diff. It is not undone from the history, and `head/1`\n    and `undoable?/3` skip it, so it does not stop the entries before it from\n    being undone.\n\nA `\"renamed\"` entry records a change of name made in names management, on\neach publication that credits the name. It is diffed like an update, but it\nis not undone from the publication's history. Renaming the name again takes\nthe change back for every publication at once. A fold of one name into\nanother is recorded the same way, so the log does not tell the two apart.\n\nTerms for renames:\n\n  * **rename** — the `\"renamed\"` entries that one call of\n    `RichardBurton.Vocabulary.rename/5` recorded, one for each publication\n    it changed. They share a `rename_id` that no other rename has.\n    `all/0` returns each rename as one entry.\n  * **name change** — what a rename changed in the fields that hold names,\n    `authors`, `original_authors` and `publishers`: for each of those fields\n    that changed, the names removed, under `from`, and the names added,\n    under `to`. A rename onto a free name removes the old name and adds the\n    new one. A fold into a name that a publication already credits only\n    removes the old name, so `to` is empty for that publication. See\n    `annotate/2`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.absorbed/1","doc":"Returns the ids among `ids` of the publications that are absorbed now.\n\nA publication is absorbed when the latest `\"merged\"` or `\"unmerged\"` entry\nthat names it in `others` is a `\"merged\"` one. That entry is in the stream\nof the publication that survived the merge, not in the absorbed\npublication's own stream. The query reads only the ids that `others` holds,\nand at most one entry for each of `ids`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#absorbed/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.all/0","doc":"Returns every entry in the log, newest first, annotated by `annotate/2`,\nwith each rename as one entry.\n\nThe entry that stands for a rename is the rename's newest entry, in the\nplace of that entry. Its `others` hold the other publications the rename\nchanged, each with the snapshot of its own entry, and its `name_change`\ncovers every entry of the rename: each field that any of them changed, with\nevery name removed and every name added, each once and sorted. Entries of\nevery other action are returned as they are.\n\nIt reads the log with one query. The publications that are absorbed are\nfound from the merge and un-merge entries among the ones it read.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#all/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.annotate/2","doc":"Sets the `diff`, `undoable` and `name_change` fields of each entry, and\nreturns the entries in the order given. `entries` come newest first within\neach publication's stream, and may hold the streams of several\npublications, interleaved.\n`absorbed` holds the ids of the publications that are absorbed.\n\nAn update, a rename, a merge and an un-merge get a diff, which compares the\nentry's snapshot with the snapshot of the next older entry in its own\nstream. It holds `fields`, each changed field with its value `from` before\nand `to` after, and `sources`, the sources added and removed, or nil when\nthe sources did not change. Every other entry, and the oldest entry of a\nstream, gets a nil diff.\n\nAn entry is undoable when `undoable?/3` says so, and never while its\npublication is absorbed, because only undoing the merge gives that\npublication back.\n\nA rename's entry gets its name change (see the module doc), computed from\nits diff. It is nil when the diff is nil or changed no field that holds\nnames. Every other entry gets a nil name change.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#annotate/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Publication.History.annotate/2","doc":"An update and the import before it, newest first:\n\n    iex> alias RichardBurton.Publication.History\n    iex> [update, import] =\n    ...>   History.annotate(\n    ...>     [\n    ...>       %History{publication_id: 1, version: 2, action: \"updated\", snapshot: %{\"title\" => \"New\"}},\n    ...>       %History{publication_id: 1, version: 1, action: \"created\", snapshot: %{\"title\" => \"Old\"}}\n    ...>     ],\n    ...>     MapSet.new()\n    ...>   )\n    iex> update.diff.fields\n    %{\"title\" => %{from: \"Old\", to: \"New\"}}\n    iex> {update.undoable, import.diff, import.undoable}\n    {true, nil, false}\n\nThe update is the head, so it is undoable. The import has no older entry to\ncompare with, and it is not undoable once a later entry exists.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#annotate/2-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.head/1","doc":"Returns the latest entry of a stream that is not a decision, or the latest\nentry when every entry is one. A decision changes no field, so the entries\nbefore it are undone as if it were not there.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#head/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.of/1","doc":"Returns the stream of one publication, annotated by `annotate/2`.\n\nEach rename's entry holds, in `others`, the other publications its rename\nchanged, each with the snapshot of its own entry of the rename. Those\nentries are read with one more query, made only when the stream holds a\nrename's entry.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#of/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.other_ids/1","doc":"Returns the ids of the other publications an entry is about.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#other_ids/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.other_records/1","doc":"Returns the snapshots of the other publications an entry is about, ordered\nby id. Each snapshot keeps its `\"id\"` and leaves out the fingerprints.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#other_records/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.record/4","doc":"Appends the entry that records `action` on `publication`, inside the\ncaller's transaction. `others` names the publications a merge absorbed, or\nan un-merge gave back.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#record/4"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.record_created/2","doc":"Appends a `\"created\"` entry for each of `publications`, with one insert per\nchunk, inside the caller's transaction. The publications must be ones the\ntransaction inserted, so each entry is its publication's first version.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#record_created/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.record_decision/3","doc":"Appends a decision's entry for each of `publications`, inside the caller's\ntransaction, with the same queries as `record_rename/2`. `action` is\n`:distinguished` or `:reconsidered`, and each entry names the other\npublications in `others`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#record_decision/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.record_rename/2","doc":"Appends a `\"renamed\"` entry for each of `publications`, inside the caller's\ntransaction. The entries share a new `rename_id`. It reads the\npublications' current versions with one query and inserts the entries with\none insert per chunk, so the number of queries does not depend on the number\nof publications.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#record_rename/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.reverted_snapshot/3","doc":"Returns the snapshot that undoing the update `entry` writes: the head's\nsnapshot, with the fields the update changed put back to their values in\n`previous`, and without the keys no editor writes. For the head itself, that\nis the snapshot of `previous`. For an older update, the later changes to\nother fields are kept.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#reverted_snapshot/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.snapshot/1","doc":"Returns the snapshot a history entry stores for `publication`: the flat\nfields `RichardBurton.Publication.Codec.flatten/1` returns for it, with\natom keys and each set of names sorted. The publication's associations must\nbe loaded.\n\nTwo snapshots are equal when the publication holds the same values, which a\nchangeset cannot tell, because it replaces the sources on every save.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#snapshot/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Publication.History.snapshot/1","doc":"iex> alias RichardBurton.{Author, Country, OriginalBook, Publication, Publisher, TranslatedBook}\n    iex> publication = %Publication{\n    ...>   title: \"Dom Casmurro\",\n    ...>   year: 1953,\n    ...>   countries: [%Country{code: \"US\"}],\n    ...>   publishers: [%Publisher{name: \"Noonday Press\"}],\n    ...>   sources: [],\n    ...>   translated_book: %TranslatedBook{\n    ...>     authors: [%Author{name: \"Helen Caldwell\"}],\n    ...>     original_book: %OriginalBook{\n    ...>       title: \"Dom Casmurro\",\n    ...>       authors: [%Author{name: \"Machado de Assis\"}]\n    ...>     }\n    ...>   }\n    ...> }\n    iex> snapshot = Publication.History.snapshot(publication)\n    iex> {snapshot[:title], snapshot[:authors], snapshot[:countries]}\n    {\"Dom Casmurro\", [\"Helen Caldwell\"], [\"US\"]}","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#snapshot/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.system_actor/0","doc":"Returns the actor recorded for a change made outside a request.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#system_actor/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.undoable?/3","doc":"Returns whether undoing `entry` would leave its publication as if the entry\nhad never happened, with every later change kept. `previous` is the entry\nbefore it in its stream, or nil, and `head` is the stream's head (see\n`head/1`).\n\n  * The head is undoable. The action that undoes it applies directly: a\n    restore undoes a delete, a delete undoes an import or a restore, and an\n    update is reverted.\n  * An older update is undoable while the publication is not deleted and\n    every field the update changed still holds the value it set. Undoing it\n    then changes only those fields, and keeps later changes to the others.\n  * An older entry of any other action is not undoable. A later restore\n    already undid an older delete, and undoing an older import or restore\n    would discard the changes that followed it. A merge or an un-merge\n    changes several publications at once, and the changes after an older\n    one were made to the merged publication.\n  * A rename is not undoable. It changed a name for every publication that\n    credits it, and renaming the name again takes it back.\n  * A decision is not undoable. It is taken back in the duplicate review.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.History.html#undoable?/3"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Import","doc":"Inserts a batch of new publications in one transaction, with a number of\nqueries that does not depend on the number of rows.\n\nThe batch is stored as its rows would be if each were imported alone, one\nafter another. A row links to the stored countries, publishers, authors and\nbooks it names, and to those an earlier row of the batch stored, and each\npublication is recorded in the history with a `created` entry. When a row is\ninvalid, or has the same composite key as a stored publication or an earlier\nrow, nothing is stored.\n\nA *row* is one entry of the batch, cast with `Publication.changeset/2`.\n\n`Publication.Links` finds or inserts the names and books that the rows use.\nThe publications, their links, their sources and their history entries are\nthen inserted with one statement per table. The composite keys are checked\nonce, after every row is written, with `Identity.settle/0`. A conflict leaves\nthe transaction usable, so the rows can still be read to find the first one\nin conflict.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Import.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Import.insert_all/2","doc":"Inserts the publications that `attrs_list` describes, and records `actor` as\nthe author of each one's `created` history entry.\n\nReturns `{:ok, publications}` in the order of `attrs_list`, each with the\nassociations `Publication.preload/1` loads.\n\nReturns `{:error, {attrs, errors}}` for the first invalid row, where\n`errors` is what `Validation.get_errors/1` returns for it, before anything is\nwritten. When every row is valid, returns `{:error, {attrs, :conflict}}` for\nthe first row with the same composite key as a stored publication or an\nearlier row.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Import.html#insert_all/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index","doc":"Full-text search over the publication index.\n\nA term is split on `:or` (or `:ou`) into **alternatives**, and a publication\nmatches if it satisfies any one of them.\n\nEach alternative contains free words and operators. Free words are matched one\nat a time and combined with AND, so every word has to match. An operator\nrestricts its value to a single field:\n\n    title:casmurro            search the title only\n    autor:machado             search the author, written in Portuguese\n    year:1950-1960            a range of years\n    -country:US               exclude\n    title:\"dom casmurro\"      these words, in this order\n\nAn alternative matches when both its words and its operators match.\n\nTerms used here, which also appear as tags in the code:\n\n  * **spelled out** — a term that uses quotes or a leading `-` but contains no\n    operator. Postgres already understands that syntax, so the term is handed\n    to `websearch_to_tsquery` as written instead of being parsed here. Tagged\n    `{:spelled_out, term}`.\n  * **criteria** — what a term becomes once it has been read: either\n    `{:spelled_out, term}` or `{:alternatives, alternatives}`. `matches/1` and\n    `ranking/1` are built from it, and it is the only thing that differs\n    between the two ways of reading a term.\n  * **listed row** — a publication as a page of the index holds it: a\n    `RichardBurton.FlatPublication` read from the view, with `excerpts` and\n    `marked`. Its `notes` are nil, whatever notes the publication has, and\n    its JSON has no `notes` key, so that a page stays small however long\n    the notes are. `details/3` and\n    `without_sources/0` return listed rows. `all/1` and `search/2` return\n    `FlatPublication` structs whose notes are read from the `publications`\n    table, or maps of the fields they are asked for.\n\nEach word is matched on its own: as a prefix when the index holds words\nbeginning with it, and by resemblance when it does not. So a word matched by\nresemblance does not change how the words beside it match, and the query is\nrun once.\n\nHow each word matched is its **standing** (see\n`RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Keywords`). `standings/1` looks up the\nstandings of all of a term's words with one query. `search_order/2`,\n`details/3` and `matching/2` take those standings along with the term, so\nthat several of them can read one lookup. Given the term alone, each of them\nlooks the standings up itself.\n\nBoth ways of reading a term match against a `tsvector` whose accents have been\nstripped, so the term has its accents stripped too before it is compared. See\n`RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term` for the vocabulary a term is written in\nand the names each operator answers to.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.all/0","doc":"Returns `{:ok, publications}`, every publication in the index, whole, with\nits notes.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#all/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.all/1","doc":"Returns `{:ok, publications}`, every publication in the index, as maps of\nthe fields in `attributes`, or whole, with their notes, when `attributes` is\nempty. The notes are read from the `publications` table in the same query.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#all/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.all_order/0","doc":"The ids of every publication, in the order the index lists them: by title, then\nby id. The equivalent of `search_order/1` for a reader who is browsing rather\nthan searching.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#all_order/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.count/0","doc":"Returns the number of publications in the index. It counts the rows the\nindex lists, so it includes a write only once the refresher has rebuilt the\nindex, as the search does.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#count/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.count_header/0","doc":"Name of the response header carrying the index's total count.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#count_header/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.detail/2","doc":"Returns what `details/2` returns for the one id `id`: a list of its row, or\nan empty list when the index does not hold it.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#detail/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.details/2","doc":"Returns the listed rows for the given ids, in that order: one page of an\nordering that `search_order/1` or `all_order/0` returned earlier.\n\nPass `nil` as the term for a plain listing, whose rows hold nil `excerpts`\nand `marked`. Pass the term itself and each row comes back with an excerpt\nof every field that matched it.\n\nAn id that is no longer in the database is left out. That is what a deletion\nlooks like after the order was fixed: a gap in the page, rather than rows\nshifting up or repeating.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#details/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.details/3","doc":"Returns what `details/2` returns, reading the standings of the term's words\nfrom `standings`, which `standings/1` returned for the term. `standings` is\nnot read when the term is `nil`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#details/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.matching/1","doc":"Returns a query for the publications a term matches, or for every publication\nwhen the term is `nil`. The query has no ordering.\n\nIt always returns a query. When the term has nothing to search for, the query\nreturns no rows, so callers can count over it without a separate check.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#matching/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.matching/2","doc":"Returns what `matching/1` returns, reading the standings of the term's words\nfrom `standings`, which `standings/1` returned for the term. `standings` is\nnot read when the term is `nil`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#matching/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.per_page/0","doc":"How many publications a page holds.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#per_page/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.search/1","doc":"Returns what `search/2` returns for `term`, with whole rows.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#search/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.search/2","doc":"Returns the publications a term matches.\n\nThe term is split on `:or` (or `:ou`) into alternatives, and a publication\nmatches if it satisfies any one of them. Within an alternative, words are\ncombined with AND, so every word has to match. So searching a full title\nreturns only that title, while \"one title :or another\" returns both.\n\nA word matches the indexed words it begins, so a half-typed word matches\nevery word it might become. A word that begins none matches the indexed\nwords it resembles, and a word that resembles none is dropped rather than\nemptying the result.\n\nIt returns `{:ok, publications}`, every match at once, in the order\n`search_order/1` gives, as maps of the fields in `attributes`, or whole,\nwith their notes, when `attributes` is empty. The notes are read from the\n`publications` table in the same query. `search_order/1` and `details/2`\nread the same matches a page at a time.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#search/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.search_order/1","doc":"The ids of every publication a term matches, in the order they should be read,\nor `:none` if the term matches nothing at all.\n\nThe order is decided once, here. The reader then pages through those ids with\n`details/2` and sees a stable list, because the order was fixed at the moment\nthe search ran. Without that, rows could shift, be skipped or appear twice as\nthe database changed between one page and the next.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#search_order/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.search_order/2","doc":"Returns what `search_order/1` returns, reading the standings of the term's\nwords from `standings`, which `standings/1` returned for the term.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#search_order/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.standings/1","doc":"Returns the standing of each word of `term` that the search looks up, keyed\nby the word, with one query. See `RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.standings/1`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#standings/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.without_sources/0","doc":"Returns `{:ok, rows}`, the listed rows of the publications in the index that\ncite no source, ordered by id, so the order is the same on every call.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.html#without_sources/0"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Excerpt","doc":"Builds the highlighted text that shows why a publication matched a search.\n\nA search result tells you which publications matched, but not what in them\nmatched. This asks Postgres for that: for each field, the part of its text\nthat the search matched, with the matched words wrapped in `[[ ]]`. The result\ngoes in the row's virtual `excerpts` map, keyed by field, and is `nil` for any\nfield the search did not match.\n\nThe highlighting is produced by the same query that decides the match, because\nonly the database holds what is needed to place it. It knows which spellings\nthe search configuration treats as the same word and where one word ends, and\nit knows which field each part of the term applied to. `title:night` has to\nhighlight the title and nothing else, and a list of matched words that does\nnot record which field each was searched in cannot express that.\n\nTerms used here:\n\n  * **excerpt** — the matching text of one field. Short fields come back in\n    full. `sources` is an array, which the search treats as one long\n    string, so it comes back as a short window around the match instead.\n  * **widening** — one word that the search matched with something other than\n    what was typed, either because the word is a prefix of several indexed\n    words or because it matched none and fell back to ones resembling it. A\n    word matched exactly is not a widening, and is not reported. See\n    `resolution/1`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Excerpt.html"},{"type":"type","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Excerpt.widening/0","doc":"One word the search matched with something other than what was typed: the word\nas typed, the indexed words it matched, and the field it was searched in. The\nfield is nil for a free word, which is searched in every field.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Excerpt.html#t:widening/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Excerpt.resolution/1","doc":"Lists the words the search matched with something other than what was typed.\n\nEach entry gives the word as it was typed, the indexed words it actually\nmatched, and the field it was searched in. The field is `nil` for a free word,\nwhich is searched in every field. Entries come back in the order the words were\ntyped.\n\nWords the search matched exactly are left out, since reporting that `machado`\nmatched `machado` adds nothing. What is worth reporting is that `Maries`\nmatched `marias`, `marie` and `mario`, because those results correspond to no\nword in the term as it was written.\n\nThis uses the same word resolution the excerpts use, so the words reported here\nare exactly the words highlighted in the rows. Two cases return nothing: a\nquoted value, which is matched exactly and so is never widened, and a term\nPostgres parses itself (see `RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query`).","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Excerpt.html#resolution/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Excerpt.resolution/2","doc":"Returns what `resolution/1` returns, reading the standings of the term's\nwords from `standings`, which `Query.standings/1` returned for the term.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Excerpt.html#resolution/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Excerpt.select/3","doc":"Adds the `excerpts` and `marked` maps to a query, reading the standings of\nthe term's words from `standings`, which `Query.standings/1` returned for the\nterm.\n\n`excerpts` holds a value for each field: the matching text of the whole\nfield, or nil where the search did not match it. `marked` holds a list for\neach list field, lined up with the field's values, holding each value's\nmatching text, or nil where that value did not match.\n\nBoth come from the term alone, read the way the search reads it. A field no\npart of the term searched gets no tsquery, and so neither an excerpt nor\nmarks.\n\n`marked.countries` holds the code of each matched country rather than marked\ntext, because naming a country needs a locale.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Excerpt.html#select/3"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Keywords","doc":"Maps the words someone typed onto the words the index actually contains.\n\n`search_keywords` is a materialized view holding every distinct word in the\nsearch documents. Terms are matched against it one word at a time, because\nneither kind of match works on anything longer: a prefix is the beginning of a\nsingle word, and trigram similarity between a word and a whole phrase drops\ntowards zero as the phrase gets longer.\n\nA word's **standing** is how it matched the indexed words, and which ones it\nmatched. It is one of two tuples:\n\n  * `{:prefix, words}` — the indexed words that start with the word as typed.\n    A word stands this way when at least one indexed word starts with it.\n  * `{:fuzzy, words}` — the indexed words that resemble the word, most similar\n    first. A word stands this way when no indexed word starts with it, and\n    `words` is empty when none resembles it either.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Keywords.html"},{"type":"type","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Keywords.standing/0","doc":"How a word matched the indexed words, as the module doc describes.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Keywords.html#t:standing/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Keywords.standings/1","doc":"Returns the standing of each of `words`, keyed by the word, looked up with one\nquery. An empty list looks nothing up.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Keywords.html#standings/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Keywords.words/1","doc":"Splits a term into the individual words it is made of.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Keywords.html#words/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Keywords.words/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Keywords.words(\"dom casmurro\")\n    [\"dom\", \"casmurro\"]\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Keywords.words(\"  dom   casmurro  \")\n    [\"dom\", \"casmurro\"]","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Keywords.html#words/1-examples"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query","doc":"Builds the query a parsed search term resolves to: the `WHERE` and\n`ORDER BY` expressions that `Publication.Index` runs for the alternatives\n`Publication.Index.Term.parse/1` returns. Nothing here reads a publication.\n\nTerms used here:\n\n  * **criteria** — a term ready to query with, and a tag the code matches on:\n    `{:spelled_out, term}` for one handed to Postgres verbatim, or\n    `{:alternatives, alternatives}` for one parsed into, per alternative, a\n    tsquery for its free words and a predicate for each of its filters.\n  * **predicate** — one condition a publication has to satisfy, held as an Ecto\n    `dynamic` so it can be combined with others and spliced into the `WHERE`.\n\nA free word is matched against the whole search document; an operator against\nthe single column it names. That is why a term carrying operators cannot be\none tsquery string, and is composed from predicates instead.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.criteria/2","doc":"A parsed term as criteria: for each alternative, the tsquery for its free words\nand the predicate of each of its filters.\n\n`standings` must hold the standing of every word that `standings/1` looks up\nfor the term.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.html#criteria/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.empty?/1","doc":"Whether criteria found anything to search for. A term whose every word resolved to\nnothing, and which carries no filter, asks for nothing at all.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.html#empty?/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.lexeme/1","doc":"Writes a word as a tsquery lexeme, in quotes, so that any punctuation in it is\nread as part of the word instead of as tsquery syntax.\n\nInside those quotes a backslash escapes whatever follows it, so backslashes and\nquotes both have to be escaped themselves. A word ending in an unescaped\nbackslash would otherwise escape the closing quote, and Postgres would reject\nthe whole tsquery.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.html#lexeme/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.lexeme/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.lexeme(\"caldwell\")\n    \"'caldwell'\"\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.lexeme(\"o'brien\")\n    \"'o''brien'\"","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.html#lexeme/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.matches/1","doc":"The `WHERE` clause for the given criteria.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.html#matches/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.ranking/1","doc":"The `ORDER BY` expression for the given criteria: how well each row matches.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.html#ranking/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.spelled_out?/1","doc":"Whether a term quotes a phrase or negates a word, which Postgres reads itself\nthrough `websearch_to_tsquery`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.html#spelled_out?/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.standings/1","doc":"Returns the standing of every word of a term that is matched against the\nindex's words, keyed by the word and looked up with one query. See\n`Keywords.standings/1`.\n\nThe words are the free words, and the words of each unquoted operator value\nthat is matched as text, which excludes a year and a country. A term that\nPostgres reads as written (see `spelled_out?/1`) looks nothing up and gets an\nempty map.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.html#standings/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.word_query/2","doc":"A word as the tsquery that matches it.\n\nReads the word's standing from `standings` and renders whichever way it\nmatched: a word that begins indexed words becomes a prefix query, and one that\nbegins none becomes the list of words it resembles. A word the index does not\nhold at all has no tsquery, and the caller decides what that means.\n\nThis is the one place a word becomes a query, so a row is highlighted by the\nsame thing that matched it.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Query.html#word_query/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Refresher","doc":"Rebuilds the materialized views the index reads: `flat_publications`, the\nflat rows the index lists, and `search_documents` and `search_keywords`,\nwhich are built from them. A write calls `refresh/0` once for each\noperation, such as a bulk insert or an update, after it commits.\n\nThe strategy comes from `config :richard_burton, :search_index_refresh`:\n\n  * `{:debounced, ms}` (the default, used in dev and prod): the signals of a\n    burst coalesce, and the views are rebuilt once after `ms` milliseconds\n    with no signal, outside any transaction, with\n    `REFRESH MATERIALIZED VIEW CONCURRENTLY`, so neither a search nor a\n    write waits for the rebuild. A search includes a write up to `ms`\n    milliseconds after it.\n  * `:sync_concurrent` (e2e): rebuild inline before `refresh/0` returns, with\n    the same `CONCURRENTLY` refresh production runs, so an end-to-end test\n    reads what it just wrote.\n  * `:sync` (test): rebuild inline with a plain `REFRESH`, because the\n    ExUnit sandbox runs each test in a transaction, where `CONCURRENTLY` is\n    not allowed.\n\nIn debounced mode the refresher also rebuilds once on boot, covering signals\nlost to a restart and data seeded while the app was down.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Refresher.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Refresher.child_spec/1","doc":"Returns a specification to start this module under a supervisor.\n\nSee `Supervisor`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Refresher.html#child_spec/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Refresher.refresh/0","doc":"Signal that the index is stale. Returns after the rebuild in sync modes.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Refresher.html#refresh/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Refresher.start_link/1","doc":"Starts the refresher, registered under `opts[:name]`, `__MODULE__` by\ndefault, or unregistered when the name is nil. `opts` may also give\n`:debounce_ms`, the quiet window, `:rebuild`, the function that rebuilds the\nviews, and `:refresh_on_boot`, whether to rebuild once on start.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Refresher.html#start_link/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.SearchDocument","doc":"A publication's search document: the `tsvector` that a search's free words\nare matched against, read from the `search_documents` materialized view.\nIts `id` is the id of the publication in `flat_publications`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.SearchDocument.html"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term","doc":"Parses a search term into the structure `Publication.Index` builds a query from.\n\nTerms used here:\n\n  * **term** — everything the reader typed, as a single string.\n  * **alternative** — a part of a term that can satisfy it on its own. `:or`\n    (or `:ou`) separates them, so a term without `:or` is one alternative.\n  * **operator** — the `field:value` form as written, such as `title:casmurro`.\n  * **filter** — an operator after parsing: the column it names, its value, and\n    whether it was quoted or negated. The operator is what gets typed; the\n    filter is what the query gets built from.\n\nAn alternative holds free words and filters, and matches only when both do.\n\n    title:casmurro          matches the title only\n    year:1950-1960          matches a year range\n    -country:US             excludes\n    title:\"dom casmurro\"    matches the words in that order\n    title:(dom casmurro)    matches both words in any order\n\nAn operator's value is matched the way a free word is, by prefix and else by\nsimilar words, unless it is quoted. A quoted value is matched exactly as\nwritten.\n\nEvery operator answers to an English name and a Portuguese one. Case and accents\nare ignored when matching them, and a two-word name may be written with either a\nhyphen or an underscore.\n\nAnything before a colon that is not a known name is not an operator at all:\n`foo:bar` is parsed as ordinary text. That way a colon typed inside a title does\nnot break the search.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.html"},{"type":"type","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.alternative/0","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.html#t:alternative/0"},{"type":"type","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.filter/0","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.html#t:filter/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.fold/1","doc":"A word in the form the index holds it: lowercased, with accents removed.\n\nOperator names are compared in this form, so `TÍTULO`, `título` and `titulo` are\nall the same name and it only has to be listed once. The search configuration's\n`unaccent` puts values into the same form, which makes this the way to tell\nwhether a word the index returned is the one that was typed.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.html#fold/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.fold/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.fold(\"Angústia\")\n    \"angustia\"","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.html#fold/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.name/1","doc":"The name used to write an operator on this field.\n\nA field answers to several names. This returns the English one, which is the\nname reported for the field and the name that can be written back into a term.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.html#name/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.name/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.name(:original_authors)\n    \"author\"\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.name(:sources)\n    \"source\"","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.html#name/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.parse/1","doc":"Parses a term into its alternatives, each holding the free words and the field\nfilters it contains. A term with no operators parses to one alternative of\nwords.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.html#parse/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.parse/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.parse(\"dom casmurro\")\n    [%{words: [\"dom\", \"casmurro\"], filters: []}]\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.parse(\"casmurro :or iracema\")\n    [%{words: [\"casmurro\"], filters: []}, %{words: [\"iracema\"], filters: []}]\n\nAn operator becomes a filter naming the column it scopes to, here through its\nPortuguese name:\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.parse(\"ano:1950\")\n    [%{words: [], filters: [%{field: :year, value: \"1950\", exact: false, negated: false}]}]\n\nA leading minus negates it, and words alongside it stay free:\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.parse(\"machado -country:US\")\n    [\n      %{\n        words: [\"machado\"],\n        filters: [%{field: :countries, value: \"US\", exact: false, negated: true}]\n      }\n    ]\n\nA quoted value is exact; a bracketed one is not:\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.parse(~s(title:\"dom casmurro\"))\n    [\n      %{\n        words: [],\n        filters: [%{field: :title, value: \"dom casmurro\", exact: true, negated: false}]\n      }\n    ]\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.parse(\"title:(dom casmurro)\")\n    [\n      %{\n        words: [],\n        filters: [%{field: :title, value: \"dom casmurro\", exact: false, negated: false}]\n      }\n    ]\n\nA prefix that names no field is read as free text rather than as an operator:\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.parse(\"foo:bar\")\n    [%{words: [\"foo:bar\"], filters: []}]","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.html#parse/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.plain?/1","doc":"Whether a term contains no operators, and so searches no field in particular.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.html#plain?/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.plain?/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.plain?(RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.parse(\"dom casmurro\"))\n    true\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.plain?(RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.parse(\"title:casmurro\"))\n    false","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.html#plain?/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.span/1","doc":"Parses a `year` operator value into `{from, to}`, where either bound may be\nnil: `1950` gives `{1950, 1950}`, `1950-1960` gives `{1950, 1960}`, `1950-`\ngives `{1950, nil}` and `-1960` gives `{nil, 1960}`. Returns `:none` for\nanything else, which the caller matches nothing against rather than dropping\nthe operator.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.html#span/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.span/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.span(\"1950\")\n    {1950, 1950}\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.span(\"1950-1960\")\n    {1950, 1960}\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.span(\"1950-\")\n    {1950, nil}\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.span(\"-1960\")\n    {nil, 1960}\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.span(\"recently\")\n    :none\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.span(\"-\")\n    :none","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Term.html#span/1-examples"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Insights","doc":"Counts the publications in the index, the distinct works and names in them,\nand a chart of them. `describe/2` counts either every publication or the ones\na search matches.\n\nThe counts are read from `flat_publications`, the same rows the index lists,\nso they agree with the index. A write is included in both only after\n`RichardBurton.Publication.Index.Refresher` refreshes the index.\n\nTerms used here, in `RichardBurton.Publication.Chart` and in\n`RichardBurton.Publication.Kind`:\n\n  * **work** — an original book, identified by its original title and\n    original authors.\n  * **translation** — one translated text of a work, identified by the work\n    and its translators. Each publication belongs to one translation. A\n    translation has several publications when the same text is published\n    again, in another year, by another publisher or in another country.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Insights.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Insights.describe/2","doc":"Returns a map of counts for the publications a term matches, or for every\npublication when the term is `nil`.\n\nThe map has these keys:\n\n  * `publications` — the number of publications.\n  * `years` — the first and the last year of publication, or `nil` when there\n    are no publications.\n  * `totals` — the number of distinct works, original authors, translators,\n    publishers and countries in the publications.\n  * `sourced` — the number of publications that cite at least one source.\n  * `chart` — what `RichardBurton.Publication.Chart.draw/2` returns for\n    `chart` over the publications.\n\nA search is resolved to the publications it matches once, and the counts are\nread from those publications. When it matches none, no other query runs.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Insights.html#describe/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Insights.describe/3","doc":"Returns what `describe/2` returns, reading the standings of the term's words\nfrom `standings`, which `RichardBurton.Publication.Index.standings/1` returned\nfor the term. `standings` is not read when the term is `nil`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Insights.html#describe/3"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Kind","doc":"Tells whether a publication is a first translation of its work, a\nretranslation or a reissue, and which earlier publications it follows.\n\nWorks and translations are those `RichardBurton.Publication.Insights`\ndefines. Publications are compared by year only. Two publications of the\nsame year count as published at the same time, whatever order they were\nentered in. Terms used here:\n\n  * **first year** — the year of the earliest publication of a work, or of a\n    translation.\n  * **first edition** — a publication of a translation in that translation's\n    first year. A translation published several times in its first year has\n    several first editions.\n  * **kind** — a publication is a `\"reissue\"` when it was published in a\n    later year than its translation's first year. Otherwise it is a first\n    edition, which is a `\"first_translation\"` when its translation's first\n    year is the work's first year, and a `\"retranslation\"` when its\n    translation's first year is later. A work has as many first translations\n    as it has publications in its first year.\n  * **follows** — the earlier publications that a retranslation or a reissue\n    comes after. A reissue follows every first edition of its translation. A\n    retranslation follows every publication of its work in the work's first\n    year, which are the work's first translations. A first translation\n    follows none.\n\nA publication's kind depends on the publications it is compared with.\n`ranked/1` compares the publications of a query with each other, and `of/1`\ncompares one publication with every publication of its work that is not\ndeleted.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Kind.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Kind.all/0","doc":"Returns the kinds: `\"first_translation\"`, `\"retranslation\"` and\n`\"reissue\"`, in that order.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Kind.html#all/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Kind.of/1","doc":"Returns the kind of `publication` and the publications it follows, as a map\nwith `kind` and `follows`. `follows` lists publications preloaded as\n`RichardBurton.Publication.with_ids/1` preloads them, in ascending order of\nid, and is empty for a first translation.\n\n`publication` is compared with every publication of its work that is not\ndeleted. They are read from the publications table rather than from the\nindex, so a publication written since the index was last refreshed is\ncounted. `publication` must not be deleted, and needs its translated book\nloaded.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Kind.html#of/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Kind.ranked/1","doc":"Returns a query with one row for each publication of `source`. Each row has\nthe publication's `id`, its `kind`, and `follows`, the ids of the\npublications it follows in ascending order. `follows` is an empty list for a\nfirst translation.\n\n`source` is a queryable of publications whose rows have an `id` and a\n`year`, such as `RichardBurton.FlatPublication` or a query of\n`RichardBurton.Publication`. Each publication is compared only with the\nother publications of `source`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Kind.html#ranked/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Links","doc":"Finds the stored rows that publications link to, and inserts the ones that\nare missing: their countries, their publishers, and their translated books,\nwith the original books and authors those link to. It works on a list of\npublications at once, with a number of queries that does not depend on the\nlength of the list.\n\nAn *entry* is a publication as `Ecto.Changeset.apply_changes/1` returns it\nfrom a valid `Publication.changeset/2`. It holds names, but no ids.\n\nAn original book is identified by its title and its authors' names, and a\ntranslated book by its original book and its translators' names, as\n`RichardBurton.Identity` describes. An entry's *book key* holds its original\ntitle, its original authors' names and its translators' names, with the\nnames sorted. So the same names in another order give the same key, and\nentries with the same key share one original book and one translated book.\n\nAnother transaction can insert the same name at the same time. The insert\nhere then waits for that transaction to finish, skips the name once it has\ncommitted, and reads the row it stored instead.\n\nBooks have no unique index to wait on, because their keys are checked when\nthe transaction ends. So before it looks the books up, `resolve/1` takes an\nadvisory lock on each original book's key, held until the transaction ends.\nAnother transaction that names one of those original books waits for the\nlock, and then finds the books this one stored. Every translated book has an\noriginal book, so the same lock covers both.\n\nNames, and then the locks, are taken in sorted order, and every name is\ninserted before any lock is taken. So two transactions that need some of the\nsame names or books wait for each other in the same order, and neither waits\nfor the other while the other waits for it.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Links.html"},{"type":"type","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Links.t/0","doc":"The stored rows one entry links to: the id of its translated book, and its\ncountries and publishers, loaded, in the order the entry lists them.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Links.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publication.Links.resolve/1","doc":"Returns the stored rows each of `entries` links to, in the order of\n`entries`, and inserts the ones that are missing, inside the caller's\ntransaction.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publication.Links.html#resolve/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Publisher","doc":"A publisher of publications. Each name is stored once. See\n`RichardBurton.Name`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publisher.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publisher.changeset/2","doc":"Casts the `name`, which is required and unique among publishers.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publisher.html#changeset/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publisher.flatten/1","doc":"Flattens publishers to their names. See `RichardBurton.Name.flatten/1`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publisher.html#flatten/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Publisher.nest/1","doc":"Nests publisher names into the maps the schema casts. See `RichardBurton.Name.nest/1`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Publisher.html#nest/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Release","doc":"Database tasks for a release, which runs without Mix.","ref":"RichardBurton.Release.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Release.migrate/0","doc":"Runs every pending migration of every repo.","ref":"RichardBurton.Release.html#migrate/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Release.rollback/2","doc":"Rolls `repo` back to the migration `version`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Release.html#rollback/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Repo","doc":"The Ecto repository, extended with `insert_in_chunks/3`, which inserts a\nlist longer than one statement can hold.","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.aggregate/3","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.aggregate/3 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#aggregate/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.aggregate/4","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.aggregate/4 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#aggregate/4"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.all/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.all/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#all/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.all_by/3","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.all_by/3 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#all_by/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.checked_out?/0","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.checked_out?/0 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#checked_out?/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.checkout/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.checkout/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#checkout/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.child_spec/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#child_spec/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.config/0","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.config/0 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#config/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.default_options/1","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.default_options/1 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#default_options/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.delete/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.delete/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#delete/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.delete!/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.delete!/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#delete!/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.delete_all/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.delete_all/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#delete_all/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.disconnect_all/2","doc":"Forces all connections in the repo pool to disconnect within the given interval.\n\nOnce this function is called, the pool will disconnect all of its connections\nas they are checked in or as they are pinged. Checked in and idle connections\nwill be randomly disconnected within the given time interval.\n\nIf the connection has a backoff configured (which is the case by default),\ndisconnecting means an attempt at a new connection will be done immediately\nafter, without starting a new process for each connection. However, if backoff\nhas been disabled, the connection process will terminate. In such cases,\ndisconnecting all connections may cause the pool supervisor to restart\ndepending on the max_restarts/max_seconds configuration of the pool,\nso you will want to set those carefully.\n\nIf you need connections to be restarted periodically, set the `:max_lifetime`\noption in your repository configuration instead.","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#disconnect_all/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.exists?/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.exists?/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#exists?/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.explain/3","doc":"Executes an EXPLAIN statement or similar for the given query according to its kind and the\nadapter in the given repository.","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#explain/3"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Repo.explain/3","doc":"# Postgres\n    iex> MyRepo.explain(:all, Post)\n    \"Seq Scan on posts p0  (cost=0.00..12.12 rows=1 width=443)\"\n\n    iex> Ecto.Adapters.SQL.explain(Repo, :all, Post)\n    \"Seq Scan on posts p0  (cost=0.00..12.12 rows=1 width=443)\"\n\n    # MySQL\n    iex> MyRepo.explain(:all, from(p in Post, where: p.title == \"title\")) |> IO.puts()\n    +----+-------------+-------+------------+------+---------------+------+---------+------+------+----------+-------------+\n    | id | select_type | table | partitions | type | possible_keys | key  | key_len | ref  | rows | filtered | Extra       |\n    +----+-------------+-------+------------+------+---------------+------+---------+------+------+----------+-------------+\n    |  1 | SIMPLE      | p0    | NULL       | ALL  | NULL          | NULL | NULL    | NULL |    1 |    100.0 | Using where |\n    +----+-------------+-------+------------+------+---------------+------+---------+------+------+----------+-------------+\n\n    # Shared opts\n    iex> MyRepo.explain(:all, Post, analyze: true, timeout: 20_000)\n    \"Seq Scan on posts p0  (cost=0.00..11.70 rows=170 width=443) (actual time=0.013..0.013 rows=0 loops=1)\\nPlanning Time: 0.031 ms\\nExecution Time: 0.021 ms\"\n\nIt's safe to execute it for updates and deletes, no data change will be committed:\n\n    iex> MyRepo.explain(Repo, :update_all, from(p in Post, update: [set: [title: \"new title\"]]))\n    \"Update on posts p0  (cost=0.00..11.70 rows=170 width=449)\\n  ->  Seq Scan on posts p0  (cost=0.00..11.70 rows=170 width=449)\"\n\nThis function is also available under the repository with name `explain`:\n\n    iex> MyRepo.explain(:all, from(p in Post, where: p.title == \"title\"))\n    \"Seq Scan on posts p0  (cost=0.00..12.12 rows=1 width=443)\\n  Filter: ((title)::text = 'title'::text)\"","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#explain/3-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"Options - RichardBurton.Repo.explain/3","doc":"Built-in adapters support passing `opts` to the EXPLAIN statement according to the following:\n\nAdapter          | Supported opts\n---------------- | --------------\nPostgrex         | `analyze`, `verbose`, `costs`, `settings`, `buffers`, `timing`, `summary`, `format`, `plan`, `wrap_in_transaction`\nMyXQL            | `format`, `wrap_in_transaction`\n\nAll options except `format` are boolean valued and default to `false`.\n\nThe allowed `format` values are `:map`, `:yaml`, and `:text`:\n  * `:map` is the deserialized JSON encoding.\n  * `:yaml` and `:text` return the result as a string.\n\nThe built-in adapters support the following formats:\n  * Postgrex: `:map`, `:yaml` and `:text`\n  * MyXQL: `:map` and `:text`\n\nThe `wrap_in_transaction` option is a boolean that controls whether the command is run inside of a\ntransaction that is rolled back. This is useful when, for example, you'd like to use `analyze: true`\non an update or delete query without modifying data. Defaults to `true`.\n\nThe `:plan` option in Postgrex can take the values `:custom` or `:fallback_generic`. When `:custom`\nis specified, the explain plan generated will consider the specific values of the query parameters\nthat are supplied. When using `:fallback_generic`, the specific values of the query parameters will\nbe ignored. `:fallback_generic` does not use PostgreSQL's built-in support for a generic explain\nplan (available as of PostgreSQL 16), but instead uses a special implementation that works for PostgreSQL\nversions 12 and above. Defaults to `:custom`.\n\nAny other value passed to `opts` will be forwarded to the underlying adapter query function, including\nshared Repo options such as `:timeout`. Non built-in adapters may have specific behaviour and you should\nconsult their documentation for more details.\n\nFor version compatibility, please check your database's documentation:\n\n  * _Postgrex_: [PostgreSQL doc](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-explain.html).\n  * _MyXQL_: [MySQL doc](https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.0/en/explain.html).","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#explain/3-options"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.get/3","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.get/3 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#get/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.get!/3","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.get!/3 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#get!/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.get_by/3","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.get_by/3 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#get_by/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.get_by!/3","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.get_by!/3 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#get_by!/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.get_dynamic_repo/0","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.get_dynamic_repo/0 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#get_dynamic_repo/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.in_transaction?/0","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.in_transaction?/0 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#in_transaction?/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.insert/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.insert/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#insert/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.insert!/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.insert!/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#insert!/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.insert_all/3","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.insert_all/3 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#insert_all/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.insert_in_chunks/3","doc":"Inserts `entries` with `insert_all/3`, as many per statement as fit within\nPostgres's limit of 65,535 parameters, and returns the rows the statements\nreturned, in the order of `entries`. It returns an empty list when `opts`\nhas no `:returning`, and sends no statement when `entries` is empty.\n\nWhen `schema_or_source` is a schema, each entry gets the values the schema\ngenerates on insert, such as its timestamps, as `insert/2` gives them. The\nentries may have different keys, and a column that an entry has no key for\ngets the column's default.\n\nEach statement holds as many entries as fit when every entry has as many\nkeys as the entry with the most keys.","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#insert_in_chunks/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.insert_or_update/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.insert_or_update/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#insert_or_update/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.insert_or_update!/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.insert_or_update!/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#insert_or_update!/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.load/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.load/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#load/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.one/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.one/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#one/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.one!/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.one!/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#one!/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.preload/3","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.preload/3 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#preload/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.prepare_query/3","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.prepare_query/3 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#prepare_query/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.prepare_transaction/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.prepare_transaction/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#prepare_transaction/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.put_dynamic_repo/1","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.put_dynamic_repo/1 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#put_dynamic_repo/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.query/3","doc":"Runs a custom SQL query.\n\nIf the query was successful, it will return an `:ok` tuple containing\na map with at least two keys:\n\n  * `:num_rows` - the number of rows affected\n  * `:rows` - the result set as a list. `nil` may be returned\n    instead of the list if the command does not yield any row\n    as result (but still yields the number of affected rows,\n    like a `delete` command without returning would)","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#query/3"},{"type":"function","title":"Options - RichardBurton.Repo.query/3","doc":"* `:log` - When false, does not log the query\n  * `:timeout` - Execute request timeout, accepts: `:infinity` (default: `15000`);","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#query/3-options"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Repo.query/3","doc":"iex> MyRepo.query(\"SELECT $1::integer + $2\", [40, 2])\n    {:ok, %{rows: [[42]], num_rows: 1}}\n\n    iex> Ecto.Adapters.SQL.query(MyRepo, \"SELECT $1::integer + $2\", [40, 2])\n    {:ok, %{rows: [[42]], num_rows: 1}}","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#query/3-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.query!/3","doc":"Same as `query/3` but returns result directly without `:ok` tuple\nand raises on invalid queries","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#query!/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.query_many/3","doc":"Runs a custom SQL query that returns multiple results on the given repo.\n\nIn case of success, it must return an `:ok` tuple containing a list of\nmaps with at least two keys:\n\n  * `:num_rows` - the number of rows affected\n\n  * `:rows` - the result set as a list. `nil` may be returned\n    instead of the list if the command does not yield any row\n    as result (but still yields the number of affected rows,\n    like a `delete` command without returning would)","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#query_many/3"},{"type":"function","title":"Options - RichardBurton.Repo.query_many/3","doc":"* `:log` - When false, does not log the query\n  * `:timeout` - Execute request timeout, accepts: `:infinity` (default: `15000`);","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#query_many/3-options"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Repo.query_many/3","doc":"iex> MyRepo.query_many(\"SELECT $1; SELECT $2;\", [40, 2])\n    {:ok, [%{rows: [[40]], num_rows: 1}, %{rows: [[2]], num_rows: 1}]}\n\n    iex> Ecto.Adapters.SQL.query_many(MyRepo, \"SELECT $1; SELECT $2;\", [40, 2])\n    {:ok, [%{rows: [[40]], num_rows: 1}, %{rows: [[2]], num_rows: 1}]}","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#query_many/3-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.query_many!/3","doc":"Same as `query_many/4` but returns result directly without `:ok` tuple\nand raises on invalid queries","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#query_many!/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.reload/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.reload/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#reload/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.reload!/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.reload!/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#reload!/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.rollback/1","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.rollback/1 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#rollback/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.start_link/1","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.start_link/1 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#start_link/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.stop/1","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.stop/1 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#stop/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.stream/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.stream/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#stream/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.to_sql/3","doc":"Converts the given query to SQL according to its kind and the\nadapter in the given repository.","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#to_sql/3"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Repo.to_sql/3","doc":"The examples below are meant for reference. Each adapter will\nreturn a different result:\n\n    iex> MyRepo.to_sql(:all, Post)\n    {\"SELECT p.id, p.title, p.inserted_at, p.created_at FROM posts as p\", []}\n\n    iex> MyRepo.to_sql(:update_all, from(p in Post, update: [set: [title: ^\"hello\"]]))\n    {\"UPDATE posts AS p SET title = $1\", [\"hello\"]}\n\n    iex> Ecto.Adapters.SQL.to_sql(:all, MyRepo, Post)\n    {\"SELECT p.id, p.title, p.inserted_at, p.created_at FROM posts as p\", []}","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#to_sql/3-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.transact/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.transact/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#transact/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.transaction/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.transaction/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#transaction/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.update/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.update/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#update/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.update!/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.update!/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#update!/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Repo.update_all/3","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Repo.update_all/3 .","ref":"RichardBurton.Repo.html#update_all/3"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.RichText","doc":"An Ecto type for rich text stored in a `jsonb` column, and functions that\nmeasure rich text and convert it to and from plain text.\n\nThese terms describe rich text. The keys of every node are strings.\n\n  * **value** — a non-empty list of blocks, in reading order, or nil when\n    there is no text.\n  * **block** — a paragraph or a list item. A paragraph is\n    `%{\"type\" => \"paragraph\", \"children\" => inlines}`. A list item is\n    `%{\"type\" => \"list-item\", \"list\" => kind, \"depth\" => depth, \"children\" => inlines}`.\n  * **kind** — the kind of list an item belongs to, `\"bulleted\"` or\n    `\"numbered\"`.\n  * **depth** — how deep a list item is, from 0 for an item of a top-level\n    list to 2. Lists are not nested in the value: a nested list is a run of\n    items one level deeper than the item before them.\n  * **inline** — a run or a link.\n  * **run** — a run of text, `%{\"text\" => text}`, with `\"bold\"` and\n    `\"italic\"` as optional booleans. A `\"\\n\"` in the text is a line break.\n  * **link** — `%{\"type\" => \"link\", \"url\" => url, \"children\" => runs}`. The\n    URL starts with `http://`, `https://` or `mailto:`, in any case, has\n    something after that, and holds no whitespace.\n\nEvery list of children holds at least one node, and a node holds no keys\nother than the ones listed for it. A value or a block is **blank** when the\ntext of its runs, the runs of its links included, is empty or only\nwhitespace.\n\nA value is **normalized** when none of its lists skips a level. The first\nlist item that is not blank after the start of the value, or after a block\nthat is not a list item, blank or not, is at depth 0. Every other list item\nthat is not blank is at most one level deeper than the last list item before\nit that is not blank. A blank list item may be at any depth.\n\nCasting a value checks the shape these terms describe, and returns nil for a\nblank value or an empty list. Any other value is returned normalized.\nCasting goes through the list items that are not blank in order, and lowers\neach one that is too deep to the deepest depth a normalized value allows.\nBlank blocks are kept as they are. A value of any other shape, such as one\nwhose nodes have atom keys, or one with more than 10,000 nodes, fails to\ncast with the validation `:malformed`.\n\nA **bare URL** is a word of plain text that `from_plain_text/1` reads as a\nlink: a web address or an email address, as that function describes.","ref":"RichardBurton.RichText.html"},{"type":"type","title":"RichardBurton.RichText.t/0","doc":"A list of blocks, as the module doc describes them.","ref":"RichardBurton.RichText.html#t:t/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.RichText.blank?/1","doc":"Returns whether `value` is nil, or holds no text other than whitespace.","ref":"RichardBurton.RichText.html#blank?/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.RichText.embed_as/1","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Type.embed_as/1 .","ref":"RichardBurton.RichText.html#embed_as/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.RichText.equal?/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Ecto.Type.equal?/2 .","ref":"RichardBurton.RichText.html#equal?/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.RichText.from_plain_text/1","doc":"Returns the rich text that plain `text` holds, or nil when `text` is nil or\nblank.\n\nLine endings are read as `\"\\n\"`, whether written as `\"\\r\\n\"`, `\"\\r\"` or\n`\"\\n\"`. One or more blank lines start a new paragraph, and a single line\nbreak inside a paragraph is a line break in its text. Each paragraph is\ntrimmed, and the whitespace at the end of each line is dropped.\n\nEach bare URL in the text becomes a link. Words are separated by\nwhitespace, line breaks included. A word is a bare URL when, without the\npunctuation at its end, any of `.,;:!?)]'\"`, it starts with `http://`,\n`https://` or `www.`, in any case, followed by a character other than a\nfull stop, or it is an email address. An email address is text, `@`, and a\ndomain with a full stop inside it, none of which holds whitespace, `@`,\n`<`, `>`, parentheses or square brackets.\n\nThe link's text is the bare URL as written, and the punctuation after it\nstays in the text that follows the link. The link goes to:\n\n  * the bare URL itself, when it starts with `http://`, `https://` or\n    `mailto:`, in any case.\n  * the bare URL after `mailto:`, when it is any other email address.\n  * the bare URL after `https://`, when it is a web address: names of\n    ASCII letters, digits, `_` and `-` joined by full stops, followed by\n    nothing, or by a path, query or fragment that starts with `/`, `?` or\n    `#`.\n\nA bare URL that is none of these stays text. No other formatting is read:\nevery block is a paragraph, and the text around the links is in runs\nwithout marks.","ref":"RichardBurton.RichText.html#from_plain_text/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.RichText.from_plain_text/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.RichText.from_plain_text(\"First edition,\\r\\nin English.\\n\\n\\nBound in cloth. \")\n    [\n      %{\"type\" => \"paragraph\", \"children\" => [%{\"text\" => \"First edition,\\nin English.\"}]},\n      %{\"type\" => \"paragraph\", \"children\" => [%{\"text\" => \"Bound in cloth.\"}]}\n    ]\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.RichText.from_plain_text(\"See www.example.com/catalogue.\")\n    [\n      %{\"type\" => \"paragraph\", \"children\" => [\n        %{\"text\" => \"See \"},\n        %{\"type\" => \"link\", \"url\" => \"https://www.example.com/catalogue\",\n          \"children\" => [%{\"text\" => \"www.example.com/catalogue\"}]},\n        %{\"text\" => \".\"}\n      ]}\n    ]\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.RichText.from_plain_text(\" \\n \")\n    nil","ref":"RichardBurton.RichText.html#from_plain_text/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.RichText.text_length/1","doc":"Returns the number of characters in the text of `value`'s runs, line breaks\nincluded, or 0 for nil. A character is a grapheme, as `String.length/1`\ncounts them.","ref":"RichardBurton.RichText.html#text_length/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.RichText.to_plain_text/1","doc":"Returns `value` as plain text, or an empty string for nil.\n\nThe blocks are written in order. Consecutive list items are on consecutive\nlines, and every other pair of blocks is separated by a blank line. Blank\nblocks are left out. A line break in a block starts a new line, and the\nwhitespace at the ends of each line is dropped, except the indentation of a\nline after a line break.\n\nA list item is written after `- ` when it is bulleted, and after `N. ` when\nit is numbered, indented by two spaces for each level of its depth. The\nlines after a line break in a list item are indented to line up with the\nitem's text. Each item is written at the depth it holds, so `value` must be\nnormalized, as every value that `cast/1` returns is.\n\nA numbered item's `N` is its place in its list. A list ends at any block\nother than a list item, blank or not, at an item less deep than its own\nitems, and at an item of the other kind at the same depth. Items deeper\nthan the list's own do not end it, and a blank item is not counted.\n\nBold and italic are dropped. A link is written as its text followed by its\nURL in parentheses, or as its text alone when the text is the URL, without\n`mailto:` for an email address. A link without text is written as its URL.","ref":"RichardBurton.RichText.html#to_plain_text/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.RichText.to_plain_text/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.RichText.to_plain_text([\n    ...>   %{\"type\" => \"paragraph\", \"children\" => [%{\"text\" => \"Signed by\"}]},\n    ...>   %{\"type\" => \"list-item\", \"list\" => \"numbered\", \"depth\" => 0,\n    ...>     \"children\" => [%{\"text\" => \"Isabel Burton\"}]},\n    ...>   %{\"type\" => \"list-item\", \"list\" => \"bulleted\", \"depth\" => 1,\n    ...>     \"children\" => [%{\"text\" => \"on the title page,\\nin ink\"}]},\n    ...>   %{\"type\" => \"list-item\", \"list\" => \"numbered\", \"depth\" => 0,\n    ...>     \"children\" => [%{\"text\" => \"Richard Burton\"}]}\n    ...> ])\n    \"Signed by\\n\\n1. Isabel Burton\\n  - on the title page,\\n    in ink\\n2. Richard Burton\"\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.RichText.to_plain_text([\n    ...>   %{\"type\" => \"paragraph\", \"children\" => [\n    ...>     %{\"text\" => \"See \"},\n    ...>     %{\"type\" => \"link\", \"url\" => \"https://example.com\", \"children\" => [%{\"text\" => \"the catalogue\"}]},\n    ...>     %{\"text\" => \" or \"},\n    ...>     %{\"type\" => \"link\", \"url\" => \"mailto:a@b.com\", \"children\" => [%{\"text\" => \"a@b.com\"}]}\n    ...>   ]}\n    ...> ])\n    \"See the catalogue (https://example.com) or a@b.com\"","ref":"RichardBurton.RichText.html#to_plain_text/1-examples"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Source","doc":"A source of a publication: a free-text entry that records where the\npublication's data was collected, so a reader can check it. A publication's\nsources are ordered by `position`, belong to it alone and are deleted with\nit, and they change with the publication's own update.","ref":"RichardBurton.Source.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Source.changeset/2","doc":"Casts a source's `content`, which is required, and its `position`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Source.html#changeset/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Source.flatten/1","doc":"Flattens sources to their contents, the reverse of `nest/1`. Takes loaded\n`Source` structs, which it orders by `position`, or a list that is already\nflat. Any other value, such as nil or an association that is not loaded,\ngives an empty list.","ref":"RichardBurton.Source.html#flatten/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Source.flatten/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Source.flatten([\n  ...>   %RichardBurton.Source{content: \"Second\", position: 1},\n  ...>   %RichardBurton.Source{content: \"First\", position: 0}\n  ...> ])\n  [\"First\", \"Second\"]\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Source.flatten(nil)\n  []","ref":"RichardBurton.Source.html#flatten/1-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Source.nest/1","doc":"Nests a flat publication's ordered list of sources into the maps\n`cast_assoc(:sources)` casts, with each `position` taken from the order.\nBlank entries are dropped instead of failing validation, and any value that\nis not a list gives no sources.","ref":"RichardBurton.Source.html#nest/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Source.nest/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Source.nest([\"First source\", \"Second source\"])\n  [%{\"content\" => \"First source\", \"position\" => 0}, %{\"content\" => \"Second source\", \"position\" => 1}]\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Source.nest([\"A source\", \"  \"])\n  [%{\"content\" => \"A source\", \"position\" => 0}]\n\n  A blank in the middle is dropped, and the positions stay contiguous. The\n  entry after it is `1`, not `2`:\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Source.nest([\"First\", \"\", \"Second\"])\n  [%{\"content\" => \"First\", \"position\" => 0}, %{\"content\" => \"Second\", \"position\" => 1}]\n\n  iex> RichardBurton.Source.nest(nil)\n  []","ref":"RichardBurton.Source.html#nest/1-examples"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.TranslatedBook","doc":"A translated book: an original book, and the people who translated it.\n\nTwo translated books of the same original book by the same translators are\nthe same book, and the database keeps only one (see\n`RichardBurton.Identity`). `authors_fingerprint` is the fingerprint of the\ntranslators' names, which the database writes, so the schema never writes it.","ref":"RichardBurton.TranslatedBook.html"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.User","doc":"An account: the identity provider's subject id, the email address it was\nmade for, and the role it holds.","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.User.all/0","doc":"Returns every account, ordered by email.","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html#all/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.User.at_least?/2","doc":"Whether `role` is `required`, or outranks it.","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html#at_least?/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.User.authorize/2","doc":"Returns `:ok` when the account with `subject_id` holds `role` or a role that\noutranks it. Returns `:error` otherwise, including when there is no such\naccount.","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html#authorize/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.User.cast_role/1","doc":"Returns `{:ok, role}` for the role named by `role`, given as a string or an\natom, and `{:error, :invalid_role}` when it names none, including when it is\nnil.","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html#cast_role/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.User.changeset/2","doc":"Casts a new account's `subject_id` and `email`, both required. The role is\nalways `:reader`, whatever `attrs` say.","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html#changeset/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.User.delete/2","doc":"Deletes a user's account and all of their sessions, on behalf of `actor`.\n`actor` is the subject id of the person asking, or `nil` from the console.\nReturns `{:ok, user}`. Like `set_role/3`, it refuses to remove your own\naccount or the last admin's, and returns `{:error, :self}` or\n`{:error, :last_admin}`.\n\nDeleting the sessions signs the person out everywhere. The sessions and the\naccount are deleted in one transaction. After the transaction commits,\n`delete/2` calls `RichardBurton.Auth.Access.changed/1`, so the person's open\ndocument channels check their access again and close.\n`Session.revoke_all/1` also calls `changed/1`, but that call happens inside\nthe transaction, too early to close them.","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html#delete/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.User.get/1","doc":"Returns the account with `subject_id`, or nil.","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html#get/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.User.get_by_email/1","doc":"Returns the account for `email`, compared without case, or nil.","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html#get_by_email/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.User.get_by_id/1","doc":"Returns the account with the database id `id`, or nil.","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html#get_by_id/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.User.insert/1","doc":"Inserts a reader account. Returns `{:ok, user}`, or `{:error, changeset}`\nwhen a field is missing or another account has the subject id.","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html#insert/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.User.role_changeset/2","doc":"Casts a change of `role`, which must be one of `roles/0`. It checks nothing\nelse. `set_role/3` changes a role and also refuses the changes that are not\nallowed.","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html#role_changeset/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.User.roles/0","doc":"The roles, least privileged first. A role admits everything the ones before it\nadmit: a contributor adds and corrects publications, an admin also decides who may.","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html#roles/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.User.set_role/3","doc":"Gives a user a role on behalf of `actor`, the subject id of whoever is\nasking, or `nil` from the console.\n\nTwo changes are refused: your own role, and the one that would leave the\ndatabase without an admin. Answers `{:error, :self}` or `{:error, :last_admin}`\nrespectively, and `{:error, :invalid_role}` for a role that is not one.","ref":"RichardBurton.User.html#set_role/3"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Util","doc":"Functions on maps and keyword lists that no single context owns.","ref":"RichardBurton.Util.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Util.deep_merge_maps/2","doc":"Merges `map2` into `map1`. A key whose value is a map in both is merged the\nsame way, and any other key takes the value in `map2`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Util.html#deep_merge_maps/2"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Util.deep_merge_maps/2","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Util.deep_merge_maps(%{a: %{b: 1, c: 2}, d: 3}, %{a: %{c: 4}, d: 5})\n    %{a: %{b: 1, c: 4}, d: 5}","ref":"RichardBurton.Util.html#deep_merge_maps/2-examples"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys/1","doc":"Returns a map, a struct, a key-value tuple or a list of them with each atom\nkey turned into a string. A struct becomes a plain map. Each element of a\nlist is converted, but the values of a map or a tuple are not.","ref":"RichardBurton.Util.html#stringify_keys/1"},{"type":"function","title":"Examples - RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys/1","doc":"iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys(%{})\n    %{}\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys(%{\"key\" => \"value\"})\n    %{\"key\" => \"value\"}\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys({\"key\", \"value\"})\n    {\"key\", \"value\"}\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys([])\n    []\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys({:key, \"value\"})\n    {\"key\", \"value\"}\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys({:key, :value})\n    {\"key\", :value}\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys([key1: \"value\", key2: 1234])\n    [{\"key1\", \"value\"}, {\"key2\", 1234}]\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys([key: :value])\n    [{\"key\", :value}]\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys([{:key1, \"value\"}, {:key2, 1234}])\n    [{\"key1\", \"value\"}, {\"key2\", 1234}]\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys([{:key1, \"value\"}, {\"key2\", 1234}])\n    [{\"key1\", \"value\"}, {\"key2\", 1234}]\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys(%{key1: \"value\", key2: 1234})\n    %{\"key1\" => \"value\", \"key2\" => 1234}\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys(%{key: :value})\n    %{\"key\" => :value}\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys(%{:key1 => \"value\", \"key2\" => 1234})\n    %{\"key1\" => \"value\", \"key2\" => 1234}\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys([{:key1, \"value\"}, %{key2: 1234}])\n    [{\"key1\", \"value\"}, %{\"key2\" => 1234}]\n\n    iex> RichardBurton.Util.stringify_keys(%{key: {:key, \"value\"}})\n    %{\"key\" => {:key, \"value\"}}","ref":"RichardBurton.Util.html#stringify_keys/1-examples"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Validation","doc":"Validations shared by several schemas, and `get_errors/1`, which reduces a\nchangeset's errors to the codes the API returns.\n\nAn error **code** is an atom that names what is wrong with a field, such as\n`:required` or `:alpha2`, instead of a sentence.","ref":"RichardBurton.Validation.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Validation.get_errors/1","doc":"Returns the errors of `changeset` as codes, in a map shaped like the\nchangeset's fields.\n\nA field with several errors is reported by its first one. A branch whose\nonly error is a unique-constraint violation becomes the single code\n`:conflict`, and a branch with a single nested map is replaced by that map,\nso the result names the innermost field that failed. An association with\nmany children is a list with one map per child, empty for a valid child.\n\nThe codes are `:required`, `:array` for a list field given something other\nthan a list, the type of a field that could not be cast to it, such as\n`:integer`, `:alpha2`, `:duplicate`, `:conflict`, `:too_long` for a value\nlonger than its field's maximum length, `:malformed` for a value whose\nshape its type refuses, such as rich text with an unknown node (see\n`RichardBurton.RichText`), and `:invalid` for any other failed validation.","ref":"RichardBurton.Validation.html#get_errors/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Validation.validate_no_duplicates/4","doc":"Rejects repeated entries in a cast association, comparing each child by\n`key`. Without this a publication carrying the same country, publisher or\nauthor twice reaches the join tables' unique indexes and raises there,\nturning a data-entry slip into a 500.\n\nErrors are reported under `field` unless `:as` names another key. Nested\nerrors collapse to their innermost map (see `get_errors/1`), so an original\nbook's `authors` would otherwise be reported under the same key as the\ntranslators. `:as` reports it under the key the flat publication uses.","ref":"RichardBurton.Validation.html#validate_no_duplicates/4"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Validation.validate_notes/1","doc":"Adds a `:notes` error to `changeset` when the text of its notes is longer\nthan 10,000 characters, as `RichardBurton.RichText.text_length/1` counts\nthem. `get_errors/1` reports it as `:too_long`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Validation.html#validate_notes/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurton.Vocabulary","doc":"Lists, checks and renames the names that publications are built from:\nauthors, who are translators or original authors, and publishers.\n\nThese names are typed by hand, so one name can be stored under two\nspellings, such as \"Penguin books\" and \"Penguin Books\", or \"Alfred A.Knopf\"\nand \"Alfred A. Knopf\". Each spelling is a separate record, and each\npublication links to only one of them.\n\nA *kind* is `\"authors\"` or `\"publishers\"`, the word the routes use for each\nschema.","ref":"RichardBurton.Vocabulary.html"},{"type":"module","title":"Renaming and folding - RichardBurton.Vocabulary","doc":"`rename/5` is the only function that changes a name. There is no separate\nmerge. Names are unique within a kind, so a rename has one of two outcomes:\n\n  * `:renamed` means the new name is free, and the record takes it.\n  * `:merged` means another record, the *keeper*, already has the new name.\n    The renamed record is *folded* into the keeper: its links move to the\n    keeper and the record is deleted.\n\nA fold cannot be undone, so `rename/5` folds only when its `folding?`\nargument is true.","ref":"RichardBurton.Vocabulary.html#module-renaming-and-folding"},{"type":"module","title":"Books that become one - RichardBurton.Vocabulary","doc":"The composite keys of books and publications include fingerprints of these\nnames, which the database recomputes when a name changes or a link moves\n(see `RichardBurton.Identity`). A publisher's name is part of a\npublication's key. An author's name is part of the key of each original\nbook they wrote and each translated book they translated.\n\nWhen a rename gives a book the same key as an older book, `rename/5` folds\nthe newer book into the older one, in the same transaction: the newer book's\ntranslations, or its publications, move to the older book, and the newer\nbook is deleted. When two original books are folded and each has a\ntranslation by the same translators, those two translations are folded into\none as well.","ref":"RichardBurton.Vocabulary.html#module-books-that-become-one"},{"type":"module","title":"Renames that would duplicate a publication - RichardBurton.Vocabulary","doc":"A rename can give two publications the same composite key: the same title,\nyear and translated book, and the same publishers and countries. This\nhappens when one publication was entered twice and the two copies differ\nonly in the spelling of a name. The composite key applies to the\npublications that are not deleted, so the database cannot store both.\n\nIn that case `rename/5` rolls back and returns\n`{:error, {:would_collide, publications}}`, listing the two publications. It\ndoes not merge them. Merging publications is done by\n`RichardBurton.Publication.merge/4`, which takes the publication to keep and\ncan be undone.\n\nDeleted publications follow the rename as well, so a restored publication\nmatches its names. They are never reported as a collision, because the\ncomposite key does not cover them.","ref":"RichardBurton.Vocabulary.html#module-renames-that-would-duplicate-a-publication"},{"type":"module","title":"History - RichardBurton.Vocabulary","doc":"A rename records a `\"renamed\"` entry in the history of each publication that\ncredits the renamed name and is not deleted, under the user who renamed it,\nin the same transaction (see `RichardBurton.Publication.History`). The\nentries of one rename share a `rename_id`. A fold records the same entries\nas a rename onto a free name. A publication whose snapshot the rename leaves\nunchanged gets no entry. A deleted publication gets none either: its history\nends with its deletion, and restoring it records it with its current names.","ref":"RichardBurton.Vocabulary.html#module-history"},{"type":"module","title":"Checking names before they are entered - RichardBurton.Vocabulary","doc":"`resemblances/2` compares names that are not stored yet with the stored\nnames. A name is *held* when a record has exactly that name. A name\n*resembles* a stored name when the two differ but their trigram similarity,\nwith accents removed, is above 0.7. A name that is neither held nor\nresembles a stored name is new.\n\nTwo publishers' names also resemble each other when one contains the other's\nwords, as \"Alfred A. Knopf\" contains \"Knopf\". Authors' names do not, because\none author record can hold two people, such as \"William L. Grossman & Helen\nCaldwell\", and each person's own name would then resemble it.","ref":"RichardBurton.Vocabulary.html#module-checking-names-before-they-are-entered"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Vocabulary.all/1","doc":"Returns every name of the given kind, sorted by name, as\n`%{id:, name:, publications:, resembles:}`.\n\n`publications` counts the publications that credit the name and are not\ndeleted. `resembles` lists the ids of the other names of this kind that the\nname resembles. Each of those ids is also in the returned list.\n\nReturns `{:error, :no_such_kind}` when the kind is unknown.","ref":"RichardBurton.Vocabulary.html#all/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Vocabulary.kinds/0","doc":"Returns the kinds of name, as the words the routes use.","ref":"RichardBurton.Vocabulary.html#kinds/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Vocabulary.rename/5","doc":"Renames the name with the given id. When another name of the same kind\nalready has the new name, folds this one into it.\n\nThe new name is trimmed. Returns `{:ok, :renamed}` when the record takes the\nnew name, and `{:ok, :merged}` when it is folded. After either, it calls\n`Refresher.refresh/0`.\n\nA fold happens only when `folding?` is true. When it is false and the name is\ntaken, nothing is written and the result is\n`{:error, {:would_fold, keeper, publications}}`. `keeper` is\n`%{id:, name:, publications:}`. `publications` lists the publications that\ncredit the renamed record and are not deleted, as `%{id:, title:, year:}`,\nsorted by title and year. A rename onto a free name needs no flag, because\nrenaming back undoes it.\n\nThe publications the rename changes get a `\"renamed\"` history entry, under\n`actor` (see the module doc).\n\nThe other errors are `{:error, {:would_collide, publications}}` (see the\nmodule doc), `{:error, :conflict}` when two books or publications still share\na composite key after the books are folded, `{:error, :blank}`,\n`{:error, :not_found}` and `{:error, :no_such_kind}`.","ref":"RichardBurton.Vocabulary.html#rename/5"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurton.Vocabulary.resemblances/2","doc":"Compares the given names with the stored names of the given kind.\n\nReturns `{:ok, entries}`, with one `%{name:, held:, resembles:}` per name.\n`held` is true when a stored name matches exactly. `resembles` lists the\nstored names that resemble it, as `%{id:, name:, publications:}`, with the\nmost used first.\n\nThe names are trimmed, blank names are dropped, and a repeated name appears\nonce. Returns `{:error, :no_such_kind}` when the kind is unknown.","ref":"RichardBurton.Vocabulary.html#resemblances/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb","doc":"The entrypoint for defining your web interface, such\nas controllers, channels and so on.\n\nThis can be used in your application as:\n\n    use RichardBurtonWeb, :controller\n\nThe definitions below will be executed for every controller,\nrouter, etc, so keep them short and clean, focused\non imports, uses and aliases.\n\nDo NOT define functions inside the quoted expressions\nbelow. Instead, define any helper function in modules\nand import those modules here.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.html"},{"type":"macro","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.__using__/1","doc":"When used, dispatch to the appropriate controller/router/channel.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.html#__using__/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.channel/0","doc":"Returns the definition of a channel.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.html#channel/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.controller/0","doc":"Returns the definition of a JSON controller. It imports `found/1` and\n`actor/1`, aliases `RichardBurtonWeb.Params`, and sends the errors an action\nreturns to `RichardBurtonWeb.FallbackController`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.html#controller/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.router/0","doc":"Returns the definition of the router.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.html#router/0"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Actor","doc":"Names the user who made a request, as the actor of the history entries the\nrequest records.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Actor.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Actor.actor/1","doc":"Returns the email of the signed-in user. It reads the user's id from the\n`subject_id` that an authentication plug assigned.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Actor.html#actor/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.CountryController","doc":"Lists the countries, named in the language of the `locale` parameter.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.CountryController.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.CountryController.index/2","doc":"Responds with the countries that `RichardBurton.Country.search/2` finds for\nthe `search` parameter, or with every country when there is no search. Each\ncountry holds its code as `id` and its name in `locale` as `label`. A missing\nor unknown locale is read as English.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.CountryController.html#index/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DevSessionController","doc":"Signs in without Google, as a development user with the role the request\nasks for, and sets the cookies of `RichardBurtonWeb.SignInCookies`.\n\nEach role has its own development user, `dev- @localhost`, so that\nsigning in as one role does not change the role of another.\n\nThe module is compiled only in the `:dev`, `:test` and `:e2e` environments,\nwhose compile paths include `dev/` (see `elixirc_paths` in `mix.exs`), and\nthe router mounts its route only in those environments.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DevSessionController.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DevSessionController.create/2","doc":"Inserts or updates the development user for the `role` parameter, signs in\nas them and responds with the user. A missing or unknown role is read as\n`admin`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DevSessionController.html#create/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentChannel","doc":"Relays one import document's changes between the people who have it open,\nand tracks who they are.\n\nThe list of documents is shared, so anyone who may edit publications may join\nany document. Joining a document that does not exist is refused with\n`not_found`.\n\nA change is an opaque Yjs update, the same bytes the document endpoints\nstore. The channel only relays changes and does not parse or store them. A\nchange that a connection misses is still in the stored updates.\n\nThe channel carries three other kinds of message:\n\n  * **presence** — who has the document open. Each connection is tracked\n    under the person the socket authenticated, with the email address stored\n    for that person, so a connection cannot claim to be someone else. Phoenix\n    untracks a connection when its process ends, including when it crashes,\n    and broadcasts the leave to the other connections.\n  * **awareness** — where each person's cursor is. The channel relays the\n    opaque Yjs awareness bytes and does not store them, because they only\n    matter while the person is connected. A connection can send anything\n    about its own cursor, but the identity paired with the cursor comes from\n    presence.\n  * **sync** — a Yjs state vector, which summarises the changes a connection\n    has. It carries the connection it is `from` and, in a reply, the one it\n    is `to`. A connection sends one when it joins, and the other connections\n    reply with the changes it lacks. This covers changes that were relayed\n    before it joined but were not yet stored.\n\n`topic/1` names a document's topic. `relay!/2` sends an update to the\nconnections on a document from outside the channel, as the same `update`\nmessage a connection relays.\n\nAfter a connection joins, the channel calls\n`RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentSocket.allowed?/2` again to check that the person\nsigned in on the connection still has access. It checks each time\n`RichardBurton.Auth.Access` broadcasts a change of access for that person,\nand every five minutes, because a session that expires is not broadcast. If\nthe check fails, the channel pushes `refused` and closes.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentChannel.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentChannel.child_spec/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentChannel.html#child_spec/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentChannel.handle_in/3","doc":"Broadcasts an `update`, `awareness` or `sync` message to the other\nconnections on the document, but not back to the sender, and ignores any\nother event.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentChannel.html#handle_in/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentChannel.relay!/2","doc":"Sends `update`, a base64 Yjs update, to the connections on the document with\nthe id `id`, as an `update` message.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentChannel.html#relay!/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentChannel.start_link/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentChannel.html#start_link/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentChannel.topic/1","doc":"Returns the topic of the document with the id `id`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentChannel.html#topic/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController","doc":"The import-document endpoints: creating, listing, renaming, archiving and\nunarchiving documents, and reading, appending and compacting their updates.\n\nThe list is shared, so no endpoint scopes it to the person asking. Updates\nare sent and returned as base64 strings in JSON, like the rest of this API.\nThe server does not parse them. See `RichardBurton.Document`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.append/2","doc":"Appends one base64 update to a document, with an optional `rows` count. A\nmissing or invalid `rows` leaves the stored count unchanged. An `update`\nthat is missing or not base64 is a 400 with `invalid_update`.\n\nWith `relay` set to true, the update is also broadcast as an `update` message\nto the connections on the document's channel, as if a connection had sent it.\nA connection relays its own edits, so `relay` is for an update from a writer\nthat is not connected to the document.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.html#append/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.archive/2","doc":"Archives a document, which takes it off the list and keeps its content.\n\nThis action serves `DELETE /documents/:id` but does not delete anything,\nbecause the rows are a record of what was prepared.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.html#archive/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.compact/2","doc":"Replaces every update up to `through` with one merged update.\n\nThe caller builds the merged update, because the server does not parse the\ncontent. `through` is the id returned by the read the merge was built from,\nand updates with a larger id are kept. See `RichardBurton.Document.compact/3`.\nA `through` that is missing or not a whole number is a 400 with\n`invalid_through`, and an `update` that is missing or not base64 one with\n`invalid_update`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.html#compact/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.create/2","doc":"Creates a document from `name` and an optional `rows`. Invalid fields are a\n400 with `errors` by field.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.html#create/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.index/2","doc":"Returns a page of documents and whether more follow it, as\n`RichardBurton.Document.page/1` does. With `archived=true` the page holds\narchived documents instead of unarchived ones. `limit` sets the page size.\n\n`after` is the cursor: the `updated_at` and `id` of the last document on the\nprevious page. Without it, the first page is returned. A malformed cursor is\na 400 with `invalid_after`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.html#index/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.show/2","doc":"Returns a document, archived or not.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.html#show/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.unarchive/2","doc":"Unarchives a document, which puts it back on the list.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.html#unarchive/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.update/2","doc":"Renames a document to `name`. A missing, blank or too long name is a 400\nwith `errors` by field.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.html#update/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.updates/2","doc":"Returns the document's updates as base64 `entries`, and `through`, the id of\nthe last update returned.\n\nApplying the entries rebuilds the document's content. A compaction built\nfrom these entries sends `through` back, so that it keeps any update\nappended after this read.\n\nWith `after`, only the updates written after that id are returned. An\n`after` that is not a whole number is a 400 with `invalid_after`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentController.html#updates/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentSocket","doc":"The socket for editing import documents live, with one\n`RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentChannel` per document.\n\nIt authenticates with the `rb-session` cookie, as HTTP requests do. The\nconnect request carries the cookie, and `RichardBurtonWeb.SessionCookie`\npasses its value to `connect/3`. Connecting counts as use of the session:\n`Session.verify/1` slides its idle timeout. The later checks use\n`Session.active?/1`, which does not.\n\nA connect request must also carry the CSRF token from the `csrf-token`\ncookie, in the auth-token header rather than the URL, and the token must be\nfor the session's person. This is the double-submit check that\n`RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.VerifyCsrf` makes for HTTP requests. A connect\nrequest sent from another origin can carry the session cookie, but its\nsender cannot read the CSRF token, so the request is refused.\n\n`allowed?/2` is checked on every connect and every join, in the same way\n`Plugs.Authorize` checks every request.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentSocket.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentSocket.allowed?/2","doc":"Returns whether the session is still active and the person still has the\ncontributor role or a higher one.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentSocket.html#allowed?/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.E2EResetController","doc":"Empties the database between end-to-end tests: it truncates every table but\nthe migration ledger, restarts the identity sequences, and refreshes the\nsearch index.\n\nEach end-to-end worker has a database of its own, so a reset empties only\nthe database of the worker that asks for it.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.E2EResetController.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.E2EResetController.create/2","doc":"Truncates every table but `schema_migrations`, refreshes the search index,\nand responds with a 204.\n\n`TRUNCATE` does not empty the materialized views the index reads, so the\nreset refreshes them the way a write does. In the e2e environment the\nrefresh finishes before `Refresher.refresh/0` returns, so the next request\nreads an empty index.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.E2EResetController.html#create/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.EmailController","doc":"The public contact form. It is the only write that needs no session, so a\nreCAPTCHA check guards it instead.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.EmailController.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.EmailController.contact/2","doc":"Sends a contact-form message, as `RichardBurton.Email.contact/1` does.\n\nInvalid fields are a 400 with `errors` by field. A message the mailer could\nnot send is a 502 with `unsent`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.EmailController.html#contact/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint","doc":"The HTTP entry point: the sockets, and the plugs every request goes through\nbefore the router, such as request parsing and CORS.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.broadcast/3","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Endpoint.broadcast/3 .","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#broadcast/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.broadcast!/3","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Endpoint.broadcast!/3 .","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#broadcast!/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.broadcast_from/4","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Endpoint.broadcast_from/4 .","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#broadcast_from/4"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.broadcast_from!/4","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Endpoint.broadcast_from!/4 .","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#broadcast_from!/4"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.call/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Plug.call/2 .","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#call/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.child_spec/1","doc":"Returns the child specification to start the endpoint\nunder a supervision tree.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#child_spec/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.config/2","doc":"Returns the endpoint configuration for `key`\n\nReturns `default` if the key does not exist.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#config/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.config_change/2","doc":"Reloads the configuration given the application environment changes.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#config_change/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.host/0","doc":"Returns the host for the given endpoint.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#host/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.init/1","doc":"Callback implementation for  Plug.init/1 .","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#init/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.local_broadcast/3","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Endpoint.local_broadcast/3 .","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#local_broadcast/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.local_broadcast_from/4","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Endpoint.local_broadcast_from/4 .","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#local_broadcast_from/4"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.path/1","doc":"Generates the path information when routing to this endpoint.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#path/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.script_name/0","doc":"Generates the script name.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#script_name/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.server_info/1","doc":"Returns the address and port that the server is running on","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#server_info/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.start_link/1","doc":"Starts the endpoint supervision tree.\n\nAll other options are merged into the endpoint configuration.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#start_link/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.static_integrity/1","doc":"Generates a base64-encoded cryptographic hash (sha512) to a static file\nin `priv/static`. Meant to be used for Subresource Integrity with CDNs.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#static_integrity/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.static_lookup/1","doc":"Returns a two item tuple with the first item being the `static_path`\nand the second item being the `static_integrity`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#static_lookup/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.static_path/1","doc":"Generates a route to a static file in `priv/static`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#static_path/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.static_url/0","doc":"Generates the static URL without any path information.\n\nIt uses the configuration under `:static_url` to generate\nsuch. It falls back to `:url` if `:static_url` is not set.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#static_url/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.struct_url/0","doc":"Generates the endpoint base URL but as a `URI` struct.\n\nIt uses the configuration under `:url` to generate such.\nUseful for manipulating the URL data and passing it to\nURL helpers.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#struct_url/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.subscribe/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Endpoint.subscribe/2 .","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#subscribe/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.unsubscribe/1","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Endpoint.unsubscribe/1 .","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#unsubscribe/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.url/0","doc":"Generates the endpoint base URL without any path information.\n\nIt uses the configuration under `:url` to generate such.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Endpoint.html#url/0"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.ErrorJSON","doc":"Renders the errors the endpoint raises, such as an unknown route or a crash,\nas a JSON body that holds the status text alone, like\n`%{errors: %{detail: \"Not Found\"}}`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.ErrorJSON.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.ErrorJSON.render/2","doc":"Returns the body for `template`, which names the status and the format, such\nas `\"404.json\"`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.ErrorJSON.html#render/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.FallbackController","doc":"Turns an error returned by a controller action into a JSON response.\n\nEvery controller sets this as its `action_fallback` (see `RichardBurtonWeb`),\nso an action's `with` can return the first error it meets. Each kind of error\nthen gets the same status and body from every endpoint.\n\n  * `{:error, :not_found}` is a 404.\n  * `{:error, :unsent}` is a 502: a mail the mail server did not accept.\n  * `{:error, reason}` with an atom is a 409 when the reason is a conflict,\n    and a 400 otherwise. The body is `%{error: reason}`.\n  * `{:error, status, reason}` responds with that status and\n    `%{error: reason}`. An action returns it when it needs a different status\n    from the one the reason normally gets.\n  * `{:error, changeset}`, or `{:error, errors}` with a map of errors by field,\n    is a 400 whose body is `%{errors: errors}`.\n\nA **conflict** is a valid request that is refused because of the current\nstate of the data, as opposed to a malformed request. The conflicts are a\nrecord that already exists (`:conflict`), a record held inside another\n(`:absorbed`), demoting the last admin (`:last_admin`), changing one's own\nrole (`:self`), and an invitation already accepted (`:accepted`) or already\npending (`:pending`).","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.FallbackController.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.FallbackController.call/2","doc":"Responds to `error` with the status and body the module doc gives it.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.FallbackController.html#call/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.FallbackController.found/1","doc":"Returns `{:ok, record}`, or `{:error, :not_found}` when `record` is `nil`.\nIt lets a lookup that returns `nil` be used in a `with`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.FallbackController.html#found/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.InsightsController","doc":"Returns the counts from `RichardBurton.Publication.Insights.describe/2` for\nevery publication in the index, or for the ones the `search` parameter\nmatches. The search is parsed the same way as an index search.\n\nThe other parameters describe the chart, as\n`RichardBurton.Publication.Chart.parse/1` reads them. A parameter that is not\nvalid is a 400 that names it.\n\nThe response also has a `matched` key. With a search, it holds\n`Publication.Index.Excerpt.resolution/1` for the search: the words the search\nmatched with something other than what was typed. Without a search, it is\n`nil`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.InsightsController.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.InsightsController.show/2","doc":"Responds with the counts, the chart and `matched`, as the module doc describes.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.InsightsController.html#show/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.InvitationController","doc":"Invitations: offering a role to an address, listing what is outstanding,\nresending, and withdrawing.\n\nAn invitation is redeemed by signing in with the address it names, so nothing\nhere creates an account.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.InvitationController.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.InvitationController.create/2","doc":"Offers a role to an address, as `RichardBurton.Invitation.invite/2` does.\n\nResponds with the `outcome`, because the three are different: someone who\nalready has an account was granted the role (200, with the `user`), someone\nnew has an invitation waiting (201, `invited`), or the invitation is waiting\nbut could not be mailed (201, `unsent`).","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.InvitationController.html#create/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.InvitationController.delete/2","doc":"Withdraws a pending invitation. An invitation already accepted is a 409 with\n`accepted`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.InvitationController.html#delete/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.InvitationController.index/2","doc":"Every invitation, pending and accepted.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.InvitationController.html#index/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.InvitationController.resend/2","doc":"Mails a pending invitation again. A mail that could not be sent is a 502\nwith `unsent`, and an invitation already accepted a 409 with `accepted`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.InvitationController.html#resend/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.NameController","doc":"Lists the names of the records of one schema with a `name` field, such as\n`RichardBurton.Author` or `RichardBurton.Publisher`. The route names the\nschema in its `:assigns`, as `schema`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.NameController.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.NameController.index/2","doc":"Responds with the names that `RichardBurton.Name.search/2` finds for the\n`search` parameter, or with every name when there is no search.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.NameController.html#index/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.OriginalBookController","doc":"Lists the original books, each with its title and its authors' names.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.OriginalBookController.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.OriginalBookController.index/2","doc":"Responds with the books that `RichardBurton.OriginalBook.search/1` finds for\nthe `search` parameter, by title or by author, or with every book when there\nis no search. Each book is flattened by `RichardBurton.OriginalBook.flatten/1`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.OriginalBookController.html#index/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Params","doc":"Reads whole numbers out of request parameters. A parameter is a string when\nit comes from the path or the query string, and can be an integer when it\ncomes from a JSON body, so each function takes either.\n\nA **whole number** here is a non-negative integer, or a string of decimal\ndigits that holds one. Ids, versions and limits are all whole numbers.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Params.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Params.id/1","doc":"Returns `{:ok, integer}` for a path segment that names a stored row, such as\nan id or a history version, and `{:error, :not_found}` when it is not a whole\nnumber.\n\nA value that is not a whole number cannot name a stored row, so it is a 404\nrather than a 400.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Params.html#id/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Params.integer/1","doc":"Returns `value` as an integer when it is a whole number, and nil otherwise,\nincluding when it is nil.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Params.html#integer/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Params.integers/1","doc":"Returns the elements of `values` that are whole numbers, as integers and in\ntheir order, and leaves out the others. Returns an empty list when `values`\nis not a list.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Params.html#integers/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authenticate.Bearer","doc":"Authenticates a request by the identity-provider token in its\n`Authorization: Bearer` header (see `RichardBurton.Auth`), and assigns the\n`:subject_id` and `:email` the token vouches for.\n\nThe email is assigned from the verified token, not read from the request, so\nan account is looked up by the provider's address rather than the caller's.\n\nA request without the header, or with a token that does not verify, is\nrefused with a 401 and the reason `unauthorized`. The response does not say\nwhich of the two it was.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authenticate.Bearer.html"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authenticate.Cookie","doc":"Authenticates a request by the app's own `rb-session` cookie (see\n`RichardBurton.Auth.Session`), and assigns the session's `:subject_id`.\n\nA request without the cookie, or with a session that is not valid, is\nrefused with a 401 and the reason `unauthorized`. The response does not say\nwhich of the two it was.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authenticate.Cookie.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authenticate.Cookie.verify/1","doc":"Returns `{:ok, session}` for the request's `rb-session` cookie when it holds\na valid session, and `:error` when there is no cookie or its session is not\nvalid. It does not halt the request.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authenticate.Cookie.html#verify/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authorize","doc":"Admits a request when the subject that an `Authenticate` plug assigned holds\nthe given role, or a role that outranks it.\n\n    plug RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authorize, role: :contributor\n\nA route names the least role it needs, so the roles above it are admitted\ntoo. The role is read on every request, so a change of role applies from the\nnext request rather than from the next sign-in.\n\nAny other request is refused with a 403 and the reason `forbidden`, including\none that reaches this plug with no subject assigned.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authorize.html"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authorize.Recaptcha","doc":"Admits a request whose `recaptcha_token` param the reCAPTCHA provider\nverifies, through `RichardBurton.Auth.Recaptcha`.\n\nA request without the param, or with a token that does not verify, is refused\nwith a 403 and the reason `invalid_captcha`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.Authorize.Recaptcha.html"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.IgnoreBlankSearch","doc":"Removes a `search` parameter that holds nothing but whitespace, so the\nrequest is answered as if it had no search.\n\nThe index answers an empty search with no publications, and a missing search\nwith all of them. Removing a blank search here gives every action that reads\nthe parameter the second answer.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.IgnoreBlankSearch.html"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.VerifyCsrf","doc":"Checks the CSRF token of a request that can change data, by the\ndouble-submit pattern. A GET, HEAD or OPTIONS request passes unchecked.\n\nAny other request must carry an `rb-csrf-token` header with a valid\n`RichardBurton.Auth.Csrf` token for the subject that `Authenticate.Cookie`\nassigned, so that plug must run first. Another site can make a browser send\nthe session cookie, but it cannot read the token to put it in the header.\nThe check adds to the protection of the `SameSite=Lax` cookie.\n\nA request that fails the check is refused with a 403 and the reason\n`invalid_csrf`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Plugs.VerifyCsrf.html"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence","doc":"Tracks who has each import document open.\n\n`RichardBurtonWeb.DocumentChannel` tracks each joined connection under the\n`subject_id` the socket authenticated, with the person's email address and\nthe connection's `client_id`. When a connection closes, or its process dies\nwithout closing cleanly, Phoenix untracks it and broadcasts the leave to the\nother connections on the document.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.child_spec/1","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.html#child_spec/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.fetch/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Presence.fetch/2 .","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.html#fetch/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.fetchers_pids/0","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.html#fetchers_pids/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.get_by_key/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Presence.get_by_key/2 .","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.html#get_by_key/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.list/1","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Presence.list/1 .","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.html#list/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.track/3","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Presence.track/3 .","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.html#track/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.track/4","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Presence.track/4 .","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.html#track/4"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.untrack/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Presence.untrack/2 .","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.html#untrack/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.untrack/3","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Presence.untrack/3 .","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.html#untrack/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.update/3","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Presence.update/3 .","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.html#update/3"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.update/4","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.Presence.update/4 .","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Presence.html#update/4"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController","doc":"The publication endpoints: listing, searching and exporting the index,\nvalidating publications, the writes (insert, update, merge, delete, restore\nand undo), the history, and the duplicate review.\n\nThe index is paged by a fixed **order** rather than by offset. The first\nresponse to a listing or a search holds the ids of every publication it\nmatches, in reading order, and the rows of the first page. A later page is\nrequested by the ids it holds.\n\nA publication read from the index, such as a row of a listing, a publication\nin a duplicate cluster, or a stored publication that a row resembles, has no\n`notes` key (see `RichardBurton.FlatPublication`). A publication that\n`show/2`, `index_deleted/2` or a write responds with holds its notes, or\n`null` when it has none.\n\nA path id that is not a whole number is a 404, as `RichardBurtonWeb.Params.id/1`\nsays. A write that would give a publication the composite key of another is a\n409 whose body holds `error: \"conflict\"` and, when the response names it,\n`publication`, the other publication.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.create_all/2","doc":"Inserts the publications in the request body, all or none, and responds with\nthem, flattened, as a 201.\n\nWhen one of them has the composite key of a stored publication or of an\nearlier one in the body, nothing is inserted, and the response is a 409 with\n`error: \"conflict\"` and `publication`, the first publication that repeats a\nkey. When one is invalid, the response is a 400 with `error: \"invalid\"`,\n`publication`, the first invalid one, and its `errors`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#create_all/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.delete/2","doc":"Deletes the publication `id`. See `Publication.delete/2`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#delete/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.distinctions/2","doc":"Responds with `entries`, the pairs of publications that `distinguish/2`\nrecorded as not duplicates. See `Publication.Duplicates.ruled_apart/0`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#distinctions/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.distinguish/2","doc":"Records that the publications with the ids in `publications` are not\nduplicates of each other, so `duplicates/2` no longer reports them together.\nNaming fewer than two is a 400 with `not_enough`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#distinguish/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.duplicates/2","doc":"Responds with the clusters of publications that look like one publication\nentered twice, likeliest first, as `entries`, and the `threshold` their\nscores are compared with. See `Publication.Duplicates.clusters/0`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#duplicates/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.export/2","doc":"Responds with a CSV attachment of the publications the `search` parameter\nmatches, or of every publication when there is no search. The `select`\nparameter lists the columns, and every column is included when it is absent.\nThe file name holds the search and the columns.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#export/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.history/2","doc":"Responds with `entries`, the history entries of the publication `id`, or of\nevery publication when there is no `id`, newest first. Without an `id`, each\nrename is one entry (see `Publication.History.all/0`).\n\nEach entry's `snapshot` holds the publication's fields after the change,\nexcept its notes. A change to the notes is in the entry's `diff`. The\nsnapshots in `others` are whole. `name_change` holds the name change of a\nrename's entry (see `Publication.History`), and is null on the entries of\nevery other action.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#history/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.index/2","doc":"Lists the publications in the index.\n\nWithout `ids`, the response holds `order`, the ids of every publication the\n`search` parameter matches, or of every publication when there is no search;\n`entries`, the rows of the first `per_page` of them; `per_page`; and\n`matched`, which is `Publication.Index.Excerpt.resolution/2` for the search,\nor nil. The words of the search are looked up once, for all three.\n\nWith `ids`, the response holds `entries` alone: the rows of those ids in the\norder given, with the excerpts of `search` when there is one. An id that is\nnot a whole number is left out. Reading later pages by id against a fixed\n`order` keeps them stable when publications are added or deleted between\nrequests.\n\nWith `unsourced`, the response holds `entries`, every publication without\nsources, ordered by id.\n\nEvery response but the one for `ids` puts the number of publications in the\nindex in the count header (see `Publication.Index.count_header/0`).\n\nEach entry is a listed row, which holds every flat field but `notes` (see\n`Publication.Index`).","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#index/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.index_deleted/2","doc":"Responds with `entries`, the publications that are deleted now, each with\nits `deleted_at`, newest deletion first. A publication that was deleted,\nrestored and deleted again is listed once. See `Publication.all_deleted/0`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#index_deleted/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.merge/2","doc":"Merges the publications with the ids in `losers` into the publication `id`,\nand responds with the merged publication, flattened. See\n`Publication.merge/4`.\n\nWhen the body holds `notes`, they become the merged publication's notes.\nThey are validated as the notes of an update are, and `null` or blank notes\nleave the merged publication without notes. Without `notes`, the merged\nnotes are the winner's followed by the losers', as `Publication.merge/4`\ndescribes.\n\nMerging a publication into itself, or naming no losers, is a 400, rather\nthan the 409 that `self` is elsewhere. A merged publication with the\ncomposite key of another publication is a 409 with `error: \"conflict\"`. A\nmerged publication that is invalid is a 400 with its `errors`. They are\n`{notes: \"malformed\"}` for notes that are not rich text, and\n`{notes: \"too_long\"}` for notes whose text is longer than 10,000\ncharacters, whether the body gave them or the merge joined them.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#merge/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.reconsider/2","doc":"Removes what `distinguish/2` recorded about the publications with the ids in\n`publications`, so `duplicates/2` can report them together again. Naming\nfewer than two is a 400 with `not_enough`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#reconsider/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.resemblances/2","doc":"Responds with `entries`, the result of `Publication.Duplicates.resemblances/1`\nfor the rows in the request body. The rows are publications that are not\nstored yet, so each entry names its row by its position in the list.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#resemblances/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.restore/2","doc":"Restores the deleted publication `id`. A body with the publication's fields\nis applied to it first, as an edit, so that a restore that would conflict can\nbe made with changes.\n\nWhen another publication that is not deleted has the same composite key, the\nresponse is a 409 with `error: \"conflict\"` and that publication. A\npublication that a merge absorbed is a 409 with `error: \"absorbed\"`, because\nonly undoing the merge brings it back.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#restore/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.show/2","doc":"Responds with the publication `id`, flattened, with its notes, its `kind`\nand `follows`. With a `search`, the publication holds the excerpts and marks\nof that search, as in the index. A deleted publication is a 404.\n\n`kind` is `\"first_translation\"`, `\"retranslation\"` or `\"reissue\"`, and\n`follows` lists the publications a retranslation or a reissue follows, in\nascending order of id. Each of them holds its `id`, `title`, `year`,\n`authors` (its translators), `publishers` and `countries`. `follows` is an\nempty list for a first translation. `Publication.Kind` defines both.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#show/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.undo/2","doc":"Undoes the history entry `version` of the publication `id`. See\n`Publication.undo/3`, which decides whether the entry can still be undone and\nhow. An entry that cannot be undone is a 409 with `error: \"conflict\"`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#undo/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.update/2","doc":"Replaces the fields of the publication `id` with the flat fields in the\nrequest body, and responds with the publication, flattened. Invalid fields\nare a 400 whose `errors` are keyed by the same flat fields.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#update/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.validate/2","doc":"Validates publications without writing them, and responds with each one and\nits `errors`, which are nil when it is valid.\n\nThe publications come from the CSV file uploaded as `csv`, or from a list in\nthe request body. A CSV file that cannot be read is a 400 whose `error` names\nthe reason, such as `invalid_escape_sequence`.\n\nWith an `id`, the request body is one publication, validated as an edit of\nthe publication `id`, so that its own composite key is not a conflict.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.PublicationController.html#validate/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Refusal","doc":"Answers a request with an error status and the JSON body `%{error: reason}`,\nand halts the connection so that no later plug or action runs.\n\nThe status says what kind of refusal it is. A 401 means that the request\ncarries no credentials, or credentials that do not verify. A request whose\ncredentials are valid but which is refused all the same, for a missing role or\na failed check, gets a 403. The reason names what was refused.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Refusal.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Refusal.refuse/3","doc":"Sends `status` with the JSON body `%{error: reason}`, and halts `conn`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Refusal.html#refuse/3"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router","doc":"The routes, grouped by what each pipeline requires: public reads, writes\nbehind a session and a role, and the development routes, which are not\ncompiled in production.\n\nRoutes match in declaration order, across scopes too. The literal paths of\nthe contributor scope, such as `/publications/history`, are declared before\nevery `/:id` path, including those of the public scope, so that they are not\nread as ids.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.api/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.html#api/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.authenticate_bearer/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.html#authenticate_bearer/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.authorize_admin/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.html#authorize_admin/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.authorize_contributor/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.html#authorize_contributor/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.authorize_recaptcha/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.html#authorize_recaptcha/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.call/2","doc":"Callback invoked by Plug on every request.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.html#call/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.files/2","doc":"","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.html#files/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.formatted_routes/1","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.VerifiedRoutes.formatted_routes/1 .","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.html#formatted_routes/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.init/1","doc":"Callback required by Plug that initializes the router\nfor serving web requests.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.html#init/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.verified_route?/2","doc":"Callback implementation for  Phoenix.VerifiedRoutes.verified_route?/2 .","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Router.html#verified_route?/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.SessionController","doc":"Signs in with an identity-provider token, and signs out.\n\nSigning in admits the person the `Authenticate.Bearer` plug assigned, through\n`RichardBurton.Invitation.admit/2`, and sets the cookies of\n`RichardBurtonWeb.SignInCookies`. The account is looked up by the assigned\nsubject and email, never by the request body, because the body is chosen by\nthe caller and the email decides which invitation, and so which role, is\ntaken up.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.SessionController.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.SessionController.create/2","doc":"Admits the assigned identity, sets the sign-in cookies and responds with the\nuser. An address that nobody invited is a 403 with `not_invited`, and a\nrequest without an assigned identity is a 401 with `unauthorized`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.SessionController.html#create/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.SessionController.delete/2","doc":"Revokes the session in the `rb-session` cookie, when there is one, and\ndeletes the sign-in cookies. `Session.revoke/1` announces the change of\naccess, so the document channels joined under the session close.\n\nIt needs no authentication, because it only ends the session the request\nitself carries.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.SessionController.html#delete/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.SessionCookie","doc":"A read-only `Plug.Session.Store` that returns the raw value of the\n`rb-session` cookie as `%{\"token\" => token}`.\n\nPhoenix passes a cookie to a socket's `connect/3` only as a session read\nthrough a `Plug.Session` store. The `rb-session` cookie holds a session token,\nnot a Plug session, so this store returns the token as it is, without\ndecoding or checking it.\n\nThe store cannot write. `put/4` and `delete/3` raise, because sessions are\ncreated and revoked by `RichardBurton.Auth.Session`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.SessionCookie.html"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.SignInCookies","doc":"Sets and deletes the two cookies of a signed-in browser:\n\n  * `rb-session`, which is http-only and holds the session token (see\n    `RichardBurton.Auth.Session`);\n  * `csrf-token`, which can be read by scripts and holds the token the\n    browser sends back in the `rb-csrf-token` header (see\n    `RichardBurton.Auth.Csrf`).\n\nBoth cookies are `SameSite=Lax` and last as long as a session. They are\n`Secure` unless the `:phx_session_tls` setting is false, which only local\ndevelopment and end-to-end tests over plain HTTP need.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.SignInCookies.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.SignInCookies.delete/1","doc":"Deletes both cookies from the browser.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.SignInCookies.html#delete/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.SignInCookies.put/2","doc":"Creates a session for `subject_id` and sets both cookies on `conn`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.SignInCookies.html#put/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Telemetry","doc":"Runs a telemetry poller for the app's own periodic measurements, and lists\nthe metrics the LiveDashboard charts: request and query durations, and the\nmemory and run queues of the VM.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Telemetry.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Telemetry.child_spec/1","doc":"Returns a specification to start this module under a supervisor.\n\nSee `Supervisor`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Telemetry.html#child_spec/1"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Telemetry.metrics/0","doc":"Returns the metrics the LiveDashboard charts.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Telemetry.html#metrics/0"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.Telemetry.start_link/1","doc":"Starts the supervisor.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.Telemetry.html#start_link/1"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.UserController","doc":"Reads the signed-in user, lists the users, and changes or removes a user's\naccess.\n\nThe writes refuse to leave the platform without an admin, and refuse to\nchange the role of the user who asks. See `RichardBurton.User.set_role/3`\nand `RichardBurton.User.delete/2`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.UserController.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.UserController.delete/2","doc":"Deletes the user `id` and revokes their sessions. Deleting the last admin is\na 409 with `last_admin`, and deleting oneself is a 409 with `self`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.UserController.html#delete/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.UserController.index/2","doc":"Responds with every user, with their role.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.UserController.html#index/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.UserController.me/2","doc":"Responds with the user the `rb-session` cookie is signed in as, or with\n`null` when there is no valid session. The route needs no authentication,\nso a request without a session gets `null` rather than a 401.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.UserController.html#me/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.UserController.update/2","doc":"Gives the user `id` the role `role`, and responds with the user. A role that\nis not one is a 400 with `invalid_role`. Demoting the last admin is a 409\nwith `last_admin`, and changing one's own role is a 409 with `self`.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.UserController.html#update/2"},{"type":"module","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.VocabularyController","doc":"Lists, checks and renames the author and publisher names that publications\nare built from. See `RichardBurton.Vocabulary`. A `kind` that\n`RichardBurton.Vocabulary.kinds/0` does not list is a 404.\n\nThere are no create or delete actions. A name is created when a publication\nthat uses it is saved. It is deleted only when a rename folds it into another\nname, and a name that no publication uses stays in the list.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.VocabularyController.html"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.VocabularyController.index/2","doc":"Responds with `entries`, every name of `kind` as\n`RichardBurton.Vocabulary.all/1` returns it, and `kinds`, the kinds there\nare.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.VocabularyController.html#index/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.VocabularyController.resemblances/2","doc":"Returns, for each name in `\"names\"`, whether it is already stored and which\nstored names it resembles. See `RichardBurton.Vocabulary.resemblances/2`.\n\nThis action writes nothing. It is a POST because the list of names can be\ntoo long for a URL.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.VocabularyController.html#resemblances/2"},{"type":"function","title":"RichardBurtonWeb.VocabularyController.update/2","doc":"Renames a name, and responds with `outcome` set to `\"renamed\"` or\n`\"merged\"`. See `RichardBurton.Vocabulary.rename/5`. The publications the\nrename changes get a history entry under the signed-in user.\n\nFolding into a name that is already taken requires `\"fold\" => true`. Without\nit, nothing is written and the response is 409 with `error: \"would_fold\"`,\n`into`, the name that has it, and `publications`, the publications that\ncredit the name being renamed. A rename that would give two publications the\nsame identity is also a 409, with `error: \"would_collide\"` and the\n`publications` involved. A blank name is a 400 with `blank`, and an unknown\nname is a 404.","ref":"RichardBurtonWeb.VocabularyController.html#update/2"}],"proglang":"elixir","content_type":"text/markdown","producer":{"name":"ex_doc","version":"0.40.4"}}